sider each condition:\n\n- We start by solving $(A-5I)^{2}v=0.$ First, we have\n\n$$\nA-5I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -12 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nComputing its square, we see that this matrix is nilpotent:\n\n$$\n(A-5I)^{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nThis means that *any* vector in ${R}^{2}$ is a solution to $(A-5I)^{2}v=0.$ Therefore, the solution to $(A-5I)^{2}v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2}\\end{aligned}]={v}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $(A-5I)v=0.$ We have\n\n$$\nA-5I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -12 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,.\n$$\n\nConverting this matrix to reduced row echelon form, we get\n\n$$\nA-5I∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis means that the solution to $(A-5I)v=0$ is ${v}_{1}=0,$ where ${v}_{2}$ is a free variable. Therefore, the solution to $(A-5I)v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\end{aligned}]={v}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing our two solutions, we see that the vector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ satisfies $(A-5I)^{2}v=0$ but does *not* satisfy $(A-5I)v=0.$  \n\n Hence, $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ of $A.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-109739/ma-source-field/ea3da38a6510d87a426a8b794d6dbeefd9c9111c2d47be9ee96bb651b71a010d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c1e72b8c-ae67-5344-93bc-3a6f89255514"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-109739"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-109830/ma-source-field/54dc1794108a93286d3ef0cf17d20ec8158b19c4a45eff677022695abd26d1be/a9b1524f2aed3ac46744b3c4cfbbd99987387330b1736eaf85cc33c056ce7ae3" :answer/id 