n of the $4$th row and $3$rd column. Therefore, ${r}_{43}=1.$\n\n$$\nE=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & \\sqrt{3} & 4 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -4 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 8 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-125593/ma-source-field/6831f863eb5f497c8eb9a540ce30078c6d68ed9ca6550a16bd37a3ddead4ddc6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ecd07ea0-767d-55f0-a61c-da49dd320f89"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-125593"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-126166/ma-source-field/c4c707edbadf5fdf6f04ee5260ab272e61a5207f7e2e45c2d56df1683fb9bd53/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "64e06afb-d2dd-5f72-8d81-a243bd9a1a36" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126166/ma-source-field/c4c707edbadf5fdf6f04ee5260ab272e61a5207f7e2e45c2d56df1683fb9bd53/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "a5274832-d6b6-57c5-9feb-13ba3e341dd6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126166/ma-source-field/c4c707edbadf5fdf6f04ee5260ab272e61a5207f7e2e45c2d56df1683fb9bd53/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer/id #uuid "a58e3553-c13d-57ab-8d41-95ded8e34f70" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126166/ma-source-field/c4c707edbadf5fdf6f04ee5260ab272e61a5207f7e2e45c2d56df1683fb9bd53/99f48c1b72e8eeab33eba2584ff0661456642ba13c82a72d98a76e8e1a07563e" :answer/id #uuid "a8dfc723-a013-5b46-bb4e-27aab59f8a2b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126166/ma-source-field/c4c707edbadf5fdf6f04ee5260ab272e61a5207f7e2e45c2d56df1683fb9bd53/64ee21896e4acdba2b7ccd5d21e7c3b0caad319fb011e671d6ce72f671b3bb6c" :answer/id #uuid "083c8d85-cec5-5988-8aec-6f02ff40ae43" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\infty " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126166/ma-source-field/c4c707edbadf5fdf6f04ee5260ab272e61a5207f7e2e45c2d56df1683fb9bd53" :answer-field/id #uuid "51bd96a8-e85b-549e-a4ee-bafda464edbc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-126166/ma-source-field/c4c707edbadf5fdf6f04ee5260ab272e61a5207f7e2e45c2d56df1683fb9bd53/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-126166/ma-source-field/c4c707edbadf5fdf6f04ee5260ab272e61a5207f7e2e45c2d56df1683fb9bd53/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-126166/ma-source-field/c4c707edbadf5fdf6f04ee5260ab272e61a5207f7e2e45c2d56df1683fb9bd53/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" "q-126166/ma-source-field/c4c707edbadf5fdf6f04ee5260ab272e61a5207f7e2e45c2d56df1683fb9bd53/99f48c1b72e8eeab33eba2584ff0661456642ba13c82a72d98a76e8e1a07563e" "q-126166/ma-source-field/c4c707edbadf5fdf6f04ee5260ab272e61a5207f7e2e45c2d56df1683fb9bd53/64ee21896e4acdba2b7ccd5d21e7c3b0caad319fb011e671d6ce72f671b3bb6c"] :answer-field/correct "q-126166/ma-source-field/c4c707edbadf5fdf6f04ee5260ab272e61a5207f7e2e45c2d56df1683fb9bd53/64ee21896e4acdba2b7ccd5d21e7c3b0caad319fb011e671d6ce72f671b3bb6c" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-126166" :question/id #uuid "153b6d05-924f-5553-b86b-f60ba5f8d6c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-126166" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2n}}{{2}^{2n}(n!)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe series converges to $f(x)$ for $|x|<R,$ where $R$ is the radius of convergence of the Taylor series. Calculate the radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ about $x=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of ${f}^{′}(x),$ we need to compute $R.$\n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{x}^{2n+2}}{{2}^{2n+2}((n+1)!)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{{2}^{2n}(n!)^{2}}{{x}^{2n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}(n+1)^{2}}| \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$ Since $0<1$ for all $x,$ we conclude that $R=\\infty .$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-126166/ma-source-field/c4c707edbadf5fdf6f04ee5260ab272e61a5207f7e2e45c2d56df1683fb9bd53"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88b9751d-450e-5ae9-bda6-1c1d136bc929"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-126166"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-126353/ma-source-field/da8cdce12518ae4af2bdab866d3effffd7f73e788be47df4fef6ed34215c96b0/df2cada8f079b9deee4bb13b5cb96d6b9ca11f7984674a5f382f39eda575542e" :answer/id #uuid "25367444-9d31-5d08-911d-e0197f9f8855" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126353/ma-source-field/da8cdce12518ae4af2bdab866d3effffd7f73e788be47df4fef6ed34215c96b0/5f163daaf664f57490ab66cbe4637a59c623e5988f17e3c937beac8252dcd746" :answer/id #uuid "2542e69d-df39-5c64-b554-6be1595617c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126353/ma-source-field/da8cdce12518ae4af2bdab866d3effffd7f73e788be47df4fef6ed34215c96b0/861450c08e4b159cdda19c7550170ed6b9bf9fb94b6ba49d2fe5e4df491b341a" :answer/id #uuid "f60fef53-a0d6-5dc0-ab21-ab9df9058607" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "C=[\\