b78e17efb52f2165421325fd636696f21ba5"] :answer-field/correct "q-178288/ma-source-field/83b86282e674a816d65e498f45850828a8bd56be4e149ac03f3e11befb2cc44c/d498494c7b78c6cac7d897157f278828bfb7eefb70b1040ad68aea97e8d69eb6" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-178288" :question/id #uuid "1dd05574-640c-5b9b-a9a5-c23bbe5e5973" :question/math-academy-id "q-178288" :question/problem "Use the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^{7}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(0.99)^{7}.$ Give your answer to four decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{7} & =1+7x+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 7\\cdot 6\\cdot {x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{7} \\\\  & =1+7x+21{x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{7}≈1+7x+21{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-0.01$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{7}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0.99)^{7} & =(1+(-0.01))^{7} \\\\  & ≈1+7(-0.01)+21(-0.01)^{2} \\\\  & =1-7(0.01)+21(0.000\\,1) \\\\  & =1-0.07+0.002\\,1 \\\\  & =0.932\\,1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto four decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-178288/ma-source-field/83b86282e674a816d65e498f45850828a8bd56be4e149ac03f3e11befb2cc44c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e430ab-a814-5613-a45d-d8cdd3c14a0f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-178288"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-180821/ma-source-field/1e615f886be468ac7799fcd262bcb37a57707317cf2cb1d8af6433931bacc702/72619ea014978827fe29c2e2acece3b33ec13379852281f6699e7db75021cef2" :answer/id #uuid "63e7c007-4203-55bb-b308-f07a7689f401" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{0,1,{0,1},{0},{1}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-180821/ma-source-field/1e615f886be468ac7799fcd262bcb37a57707317cf2cb1d8af6433931bacc702/8b08b66cad4c4