dc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" "q-324062/ma-source-field/197f61a1119f21afc9f0440b5e826b68d13861c047935bea4cfb18cdef4172f7/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" "q-324062/ma-source-field/197f61a1119f21afc9f0440b5e826b68d13861c047935bea4cfb18cdef4172f7/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af"] :answer-field/correct "q-324062/ma-source-field/197f61a1119f21afc9f0440b5e826b68d13861c047935bea4cfb18cdef4172f7/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/id #uuid "4e0eb5a0-8dd3-54db-baaf-7d7b326d8b0a"] :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2xy,\\,y(1)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the modified Euler method. What entry should be placed in the cell indicated in the table below, if the step size is $Δx=1?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $2$ | $\\,?\\,$ |  |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe Modified Euler Method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the initially predicted slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2xy \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ |  |  |  |\n\nThe coordinates of the initially predicted new point given by the predictor equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{\\text{pred}} & =y+Δ{y}_{\\text{pred}}=1+2=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ |  |  |\n\nSo, the predicted slope at this point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =2\\cdot {x}_{\\text{new}}\\cdot {y}_{p} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ | $12$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2+12)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 14 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ | $12$ | $7$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=2.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy=1+7=8.\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ | $12$ | $7$ |\n| $2$ | $8$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈8.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-324062/ma-source-field/197f61a1119f21afc9f0440b5e826b68d13861c047935bea4cfb18cdef4172f7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-326236/ma-source-field/46fd697db6b113526b1c6434cd86d8455aebb543b3395b487f59c6ee8a10cd44/5ab3573096b30d3c5d3c378e667364cbb062f921e9b1002441e594f0774a669d" :answer/id #uuid "bd4fc918-4e9b-5f09-b20e-cf2be22cf7ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-326236/ma-source-field/46fd697db6b113526b1c6434cd86d8455aebb543b3395b487f59c6ee8a10cd44/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "56280ebc-3607-5e92-83e5-9a26a408be13" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-326236/ma-source-field/46fd697db6b113526b1c6434cd86d8455aebb543b3395b487f59c6ee8a10cd44/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "ab281b2e-5a53-5672-8da4-e676a03d7e36" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-326236/ma-source-field/46fd697db6b113526b1c6434cd86d8455aebb543b3395b487f59c6ee8a10cd44/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" :answer/id #uuid "dd0ec4cd-48eb-5345-9138-6da28aeb3e9b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-326236/ma-source-field/46fd697db6b113526b1c6434cd86d8455aebb543b3395b487f59c6ee8a10cd44/523b37883474afade764165f552bc128b7640323ff7244275508d19e6f99b277" :answer/id #uuid "c1570ae2-7522-574c-b09e-cc0720cebf4e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-326236/ma-source-field/46fd697db6b113526b1c6434cd86d8455aebb543b3395b487f59c6ee8a10cd44" :answer-field/id #uuid "12200322-ef19-5f83-936e-a28151c6b492" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-326236/ma-source-field/46fd697db6b113526b1c6434cd86d8455aebb543b3395b487f59c6ee8a10cd44/5ab3573096b30d3c5d3c378e667364cbb062f921e9b1002441e594f0774a669d" "q-326236/ma-source-field/46fd697db6b113526b1c6434cd86d8455aebb543b3395b487f59c6ee8a10cd44/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-326236/ma-source-field/46fd697db6b113526b1c6434cd86d8455aebb543b3395b487f59c6ee8a10cd44/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-326236/ma-source-field/46fd697db6b113526b1c6434cd86d8455aebb543b3395b487f59c6ee8a10cd44/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" "q-326236/ma-source-field/46fd697db6b113526b1c6434cd86d8455aebb543b3395b487f59c6ee8a10cd44/523b37883474afade764165f552bc128b7640323ff7244275508d19e6f99b277"] :answer-field/correct "q-326236/ma-source-field/46fd697db6b113526b1c6434cd86d8455aebb543b3395b487f59c6ee8a10cd44/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-326236" :question/id #uuid "17c5a462-c29c-5b82-b891-ee8c95c1220f" :question/math-academy-id "q-326236" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince $A$ is lower-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-1$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-1-(-1) & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1-(-1)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=0,b=\\frac{1}{3},$ and $a+b=\\frac{1}{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-326236/ma-source-field/46fd697db6b113526b1c6434cd86d8455aebb543b3395b487f59c6ee8a10cd44"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c19f9d9-fa4b-5261-b9a0-99a203aeac30"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-326236"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186152568 :question/answer-fields 17592186149815] {:db/id "q-331249/ma-source-field/5d77dcb58e0f47be257de419944f6ca86705551942298810d43df4a67e7c2001/a53f8f56e4d6fe633508088e423689b14d5f81f503c341afd9581ac71ae97780" :answer/id #uuid "86616aa8-cac3-5e38-abcb-b93049bf8fc0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2u(t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331249/ma-source-field/5d77dcb58e0f47be257de419944f6ca86705551942298810d43df4a67e7c2001/e0d71f12378d73ea1fba85d5e027e7098f03cd40e9f5c0b66f9076e3974f81e9" :answer/id #uuid "ecf85836-8af9-5dd0-b861-b1b317d9d367" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2u(t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331249/ma-source-field/5d77dcb58e0f47be257de419944f6ca86705551942298810d43df4a67e7c2001/16c56e9fdf76af325494d4c22f1d4912f7ce0ed0ab8761d0a21e524187a93953" :answer/id #uuid "8e8cb577-70f1-5064-b674-0f4bc439a149" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2u(t)+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331249/ma-source-field/5d77dcb58e0f47be257de419944f6ca86705551942298810d43df4a67e7c2001/860efc4fc7ca904e76025a2ad9def958d3f156d5a46bfc9b4fb739fb4c332b9f" :answer/id #uuid "f8d6e7bd-c41a-54f0-8382-605057089f06" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2u(t-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331249/ma-source-field/5d77dcb58e0f47be257de419944f6ca86705551942298810d43df4a67e7c2001/e7c081d5bd299d88b7819bc4fadb624f5b0615445b47a9f3248ea670404ad7f8" :answer/id #uuid "068e5e02-b893-53b4-ab74-bb31ec326ddd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "u(t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331249/ma-source-field/5d77dcb58e0f47be257de419944f6ca86705551942298810d43df4a67e7c2001" :answer-field/id #uuid "0083f89c-06cf-585e-a9ce-c98516fb42e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-331249/ma-source-field/5d77dcb58e0f47be257de419944f6ca86705551942298810d43df4a67e7c2001/a53f8f56e4d6fe633508088e423689b14d5f81f503c341afd9581ac71ae97780" "q-331249/ma-source-field/5d77dcb58e0f47be257de419944f6ca86705551942298810d43df4a67e7c2001/e0d71f12378d73ea1fba85d5e027e7098f03cd40e9f5c0b66f9076e3974f81e9" "q-331249/ma-source-field/5d77dcb58e0f47be257de419944f6ca86705551942298810d43df4a67e7c2001/16c56e9fdf76af325494d4c22f1d4912f7ce0ed0ab8761d0a21e524187a93953" "q-331249/ma-source-field/5d77dcb58e0f47be257de419944f6ca86705551942298810d43df4a67e7c2001/860efc4fc7ca904e76025a2ad9def958d3f156d5a46bfc9b4fb739fb4c332b9f" "q-331249/ma-source-field/5d77dcb58e0f47be257de419944f6ca86705551942298810d43df4a67e7c2001/e7c081d5bd299d88b7819bc4fadb624f5b0615445b47a9f3248ea670404ad7f8"] :answer-field/correct "q-331249/ma-source-field/5d77dcb58e0f47be257de419944f6ca86705551942298810d43df4a67e7c2001/e0d71f12378d73ea1fba85d5e027e7098f03cd40e9f5c0b66f9076e3974f81e9" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/id #uuid "93d8abe5-b394-5689-9e86-76b2052077e7"] :question/problem "![](images/bc/bcf4076849dd96858c586f8696414789b3f68074b87652601245407142f2b8fc.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(t)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be $f(t),$ where $u$ denotes the unit step function?" :question/worked-solution "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nu(t)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/9f/9fb8c20672705e9d68e69d32e7ff3499ec71560e10aa85136b3f3ad75c5fb5ed.png)\n\nNotice that to transform $y=u(t)$ to the given function $y=f(t),$ we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we scale $y=u(t)$ by a factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis, giving $y=2u(t).$\n\n- There is no horizontal shift or offset.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(t)=2u(t).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-331249/ma-source-field/5d77dcb58e0f47be257de419944f6ca86705551942298810d43df4a67e7c2001"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-331263/ma-source-field/bc777f7984b499c5693dcaecdebb80f2a7c4f197b6ec5cd5b5e17d9a93416088/61f94b67bc91