n "The highest possible degree of $P+Q$ is $7.$\n\nFor example, suppose $P={x}^{7}$ and $Q={x}^{7}.$ Then $P+Q=2{x}^{7}.$ \n\nSince neither $P$ nor $Q$ contain terms of order higher than ${x}^{7},$ $P+Q$ can't contain terms of order higher than ${x}^{7}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-46423/ma-source-field/ef46ba0de2236c63a4a4b8a77d3ec36cc8e0891e6dfebec0cd41861fe3d18092"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36318568-6dfe-5ba9-b607-a2d269d05425"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-46423"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-47077/ma-source-field/df30962212e56d965451fdd867c709cbbfae0da6db7d3269eb29fe9124ebf3dd/8406158c0997d20670e2a0b495f23aaf50f27fbfe1747535b5e0d43156109f26" :answer/id #uuid "586fee85-5bd1-5bd9-b903-3f988c962fea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,3]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47077/ma-source-field/df30962212e56d965451fdd867c709cbbfae0da6db7d3269eb29fe9124ebf3dd/289da39c1b1637e8c35f310f18d8d0161f55139ea91e17d74c032f9b7844a8b9" :answer/id #uuid "5ff46e30-81e1-52bf-b55f-724406f58c7f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[-2,0]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47077/ma-source-field/df30962212e56d965451fdd867c709cbbfae0da6db7d3269eb29fe9124ebf3dd/f92af53e1021b91c6026b26979abf2a4d8b656043c59928b749366d16f0d4b08" :answer/id #uuid "7b8bb4af-dfd9-5eef-baf3-2724ee4fd9e8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[-2,1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47077/ma-source-field/df30962212e56d965451fdd867c709cbbfae0da6db7d3269eb29fe9124ebf3dd/36504b8a59301b3a74959b47de0f45427b71e308359f3c738bbfe095208cc6f1" :answer/id #uuid "9c6c153e-3504-5702-bfa8-a1175ee38323" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[-2,1]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47077/ma-source-field/df30962212e56d965451fdd867c709cbbfae0da6db7d3269eb29fe9124ebf3dd/efaf615f81b16e3ebf719aebc65dc121df2380a0a056e43e76b33c5dbef23845" :answer/id #uuid "89bd78d0-e4f8-53a4-8ab8-3044e809bf86" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[0,3]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47077/ma-source-field/df30962212e56d965451fdd867c709cbbfae0da6db7d3269eb29fe9124ebf3dd" :answer-field/id #uuid "b49fc3fc-44cb-5db2-9fc5-ed9e471c021f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-47077/ma-source-field/df30962212e56d965451fdd867c709cbbfae0da6db7d3269eb29fe9124ebf3dd/8406158c0997d20670e2a0b495f23aaf50f27fbfe1747535b5e0d43156109f26" "q-47077/ma-source-field/df30962212e56d965451fdd867c709cbbfae0da6db7d3269eb29fe9124ebf3dd/289da39c1b1637e8c35f310f18d8d0161f55139ea91e17d74c032f9b7844a8b9" "q-47077/ma-source-field/df30962212e56d965451fdd867c709cbbfae0da6db7d3269eb29fe9124ebf3dd/f92af53e1021b91c6026b26979abf2a4d8b656043c59928b749366d16f0d4b08" "q-47077/ma-source-field/df30962212e56d965451fdd867c709cbbfae0da6db7d3269eb29fe9124ebf3dd/36504b8a59301b3a74959b47de0f45427b71e308359f3c738bbfe095208cc6f1" "q-47077/ma-source-field/df30962212e56d965451fdd867c709cbbfae0da6db7d3269eb29fe9124ebf3dd/efaf615f81b16e3ebf719aebc65dc121df2380a0a056e43e76b33c5dbef23845"] :answer-field/correct "q-47077/ma-source-field/df30962212e56d965451fdd867c709cbbfae0da6db7d3269eb29fe9124ebf3dd/289da39c1b1637e8c35f310f18d8d0161f55139ea91e17d74c032f9b7844a8b9" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-47077" :question/id #uuid "4f66078a-9fc7-5c7e-ae29-7151d1232eff" :question/math-academy-id "q-47077" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x+1, & x\\le 1 \\\\ 5-2x, & x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the intermediate value theorem be applied to the function?" :question/worked-solution "The intermediate value theorem can be applied to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nThe function is discontinuous at $x=1.$ To see this, we note that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=2\\ne 3={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe conclude that the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain the point $x=1.$ \n\nFrom the given options, only the interval $[-2,0]$ is allowed.  The intervals $(1,3]$ and $[-2,1)$ are not closed, $[-2,1]$ and $[0,3]$ contain the point $x=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-47077/ma-source-field/df30962212e56d965451fdd867c709cbbfae0da6db7d3269eb29fe9124ebf3dd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1012ae9a-26d6-50bf-a46a-7c859d856752"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-47077"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-47101/ma-source-field/1cab7c0d5e2e7230afaa2a1179a41db965c219261fa428ed35a22640a80182cc/b552fda7f0f1aace343d10d4d0d3239e5033799e33a5b6c76d831738f490dc67" :answer/id #uuid "958e4525-323d-5584-adaa-91a9e8036aac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k=-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47101/ma-source-field/1cab7c0d5e2e7230afaa2a1179a41db965c219261fa428ed35a22640a80182cc/ac9ae2052cc2e067abcad4ecf5a3f3d17a02fa2d9a881931a644b74ccc7546a6" :answer/id #uuid "af392d2a-05d0-5dd2-abb7-493478be4513" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k=-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47101/ma-source-field/1cab7c0d5e2e7230afaa2a1179a41db965c219261fa428ed35a22640a80182cc/14da2cde52e2b4cbd5f8d3cfe2263091da377e92d2867ae7725d0715627ee24a" :answer/id #uuid "b3cb48f6-7226-52f0-b6e9-cc9be278a789" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k=-8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47101/ma-source-field/1cab7c0d5e2e7230afaa2a1179a41db965c219261fa428ed35a22640a80182cc/f9a36dc20554097250ff0324e179977bb892219ae9b27b5c2ca8864aac6c0450" :answer/id #uuid "ae811028-5105-53e1-bc94-6774288d6c46" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47101/ma-source-field/1cab7c0d5e2e7230afaa2a1179a41db965c219261fa428ed35a22640a80182cc/a80a330cf07901aff7b5aa904c4a3541c2351751eef1834664dea83e123d76ff" :answer/id #uuid "b3570634-e445-5816-bd14-931c93860185" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47101/ma-source-field/1cab7c0d5e2e7230afaa2a1179a41db965c219261fa428ed35a22640a80182cc" :answer-field/id #uuid "9fa48aaf-f754-52f3-a0c5-5c38dac2c7d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-47101/ma-source-field/1cab7c0d5e2e7230afaa2a1179a41db965c219261fa428ed35a22640a80182cc/b552fda7f0f1aace343d10d4d0d3239e5033799e33a5b6c76d831738f490dc67" "q-47101/ma-source-field/1cab7c0d5e2e7230afaa2a1179a41db965c219261fa428ed35a22640a80182cc/ac9ae2052cc2e067abcad4ecf5a3f3d17a02fa2d9a881931a644b74ccc7546a6" "q-47101/ma-source-field/1cab7c0d5e2e7230afaa2a1179a41db965c219261fa428ed35a22640a80182cc/14da2cde52e2b4cbd5f8d3cfe2263091da377e92d2867ae7725d0715627ee24a" "q-47101/ma-source-field/1cab7c0d5e2e7230afaa2a1179a41db965c219261fa428ed35a22640a80182cc/f9a36dc20554097250ff0324e179977bb892219ae9b27b5c2ca8864aac6c0450" "q-47101/ma-source-field/1cab7c0d5e2e7230afaa2a1179a41db965c219261fa428ed35a22640a80182cc/a80a330cf07901aff7b5aa904c4a3541c2351751eef1834664dea83e123d76ff"] :answer-field/correct "q-47101/ma-source-field/1cab7c0d5e2e7230afaa2a1179a41db965c219261fa428ed35a22640a80182cc/a80a330cf07901aff7b5aa904c4a3541c2351751eef1834664dea83e123d76ff" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-47101" :question/id #uuid "4f05bf34-eff5-5d2f-b84c-b025710dd1a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-47101" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x)=(x-2)(x+2)(x-5).$ For which of the following values of $k$ does the intermediate value theorem **not** guarantee that $g(x)=k$ has a solution on the interval $[2,4]?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the function $g(x)$ is continuous on $[2,4]$, the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $g(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $g(2)$ and $g(4).$\n\nSince $g(2)=0$ and $g(4)=-12,$ we must have \n$k\\in [-12,0].$\n\nAmong the given options, only $k=1$ does not belong to the interval $[-12,0].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-47101/ma-source-field/1cab7c0d5e2e7230afaa2a1179a41db965c219261fa428ed35a22640a80182cc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6388d315-340b-56a0-bc93-e380bfbf6ed3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-47101"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-48234/ma-source-field/9b8df20663503b64665ac0dbf3927ba72bb661f00c3b651cb645b94606bedeb1/d4d9d0c58dac6fecb6615f55a16d9d3e84ce77e12a45bac652d7b13344a08019" :answer/id #uuid "b32f9c1b-732e-5499-a950-ae4e40e8d8a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-2,4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48234/ma-source-field/9b8df20663503b64665ac0dbf3927ba72bb661f00c3b651cb645b94606bedeb1/88ae715561ee5b2df418683d8a7e4063045291171e7f92f28dbf20f0885b337e" :answer/id #uuid "0db6826b-749e-538b-898e-9f8e45963932" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-4,-2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48234/ma-source-field/9b8df20663503b64665ac0dbf3927ba72bb661f00c3b651cb645b94606bedeb1/3eaf4456135b03d48305ee199e5955cbf4f827eef281a042416764c926abc1aa" :answer/id #uuid "d9c706c6-a7cb-5cbf-a31c-59d0e18e0111" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48234/ma-source-field/9b8df20663503b64665ac0dbf3927ba72bb661f00c3b651cb645b94606bedeb1/c700f8d11f8efcb4d5adaca4943307ec7beb32a12cb40d1036833097d1b89caa" :answer/id #uuid "b3965102-b7bf-58c1-9626-978cbd7ed7aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\infty ,1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48234/ma-source-field/9b8df20663503b64665ac0dbf3927ba72bb661f00c3b651cb645b94606bedeb1/05fd2b707954215b6e31003f3b5719ea407ae62ffb7fc927134bd7a3648b1020" :answer/id #uuid "225115ed-fe14-54a2-9ff7-14b01a6ca201" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48234/ma-source-field/9b8df20663503b64665ac0dbf3927ba72bb661f00c3b651cb645b94606bedeb1" :answer-field/id #uuid "134c28b1-b3ff-5d06-b647-ccf5e9b6bae2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-48234/ma-source-field/9b8df20663503b64665ac0dbf3927ba72bb661f00c3b651cb645b94606bedeb1/d4d9d0c58dac6fecb6615f55a16d9d3e84ce77e12a45bac652d7b13344a08019" "q-48234/ma-source-field/9b8df20663503b64665ac0dbf3927ba72bb661f00c3b651cb645b94606bedeb1/88ae715561ee5b2df418683d8a7e4063045291171e7f92f28dbf20f0885b337e" "q-48234/ma-source-field/9b8df20663503b64665ac0dbf3927ba72bb661f00c3b651cb645b94606bedeb1/3eaf4456135b03d48305ee199e5955cbf4f827eef281a042416764c926abc1aa" "q-48234/ma-source-field/9b8df20663503b64665ac0dbf3927ba72bb661f00c3b651cb645b94606bedeb1/c700f8d11f8efcb4d5adaca4943307ec7beb32a12cb40d1036833097d1b89caa" "q-48234/ma-source-field/9b8df20663503b64665ac0dbf3927ba72bb661f00c3b651cb645b94606bedeb1/05fd2b707954215b6e31003f3b5719ea407ae62ffb7fc927134bd7a3648b1020"] :answer-field/correct "q-48234/ma-source-field/9b8df20663503b64665ac0dbf3927ba72bb661f00c3b651cb645b94606bedeb1/3eaf4456135b03d48305ee199e5955cbf4f827eef281a042416764c926abc1aa" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-48234" :question/id #uuid "8b5e7d86-6590-5293-af9e-465db1c9211b" :question/math-academy-id "q-48234" :question/problem "![](images/43/43786cb577705d6ae13d44e75203fef441905e9ccfc3713726f07d36c147c7b3.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $y=f(x)$ above continuous? You may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." :question/worked-solution "To determine the intervals of continuity of a function, we take the domain of the function and exclude any points of discontinuity. \n\nThe function is defined on the interval $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ The function is discontinuous at $x=-3$ and at $x=3,$ so we need to exclude these points..\n\nTherefore, the function is continuous on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-3),$ $(-3,3),$ and $(-3,\\infty ).$  It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals.\n\nAmong the answer choices, the correct one is the following: \n\n$\\,$ $(-\\infty ,-3)$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $(-4,-2)$ and $(-\\infty ,1)$ contain the excluded point $-3,$  so the function is not continuous on these intervals.\n\n- $(-2,4)$ and $(2,\\infty )$ contain the excluded point $3,$ so the function is not continuous on these intervals." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-48234/ma-source-field/9b8df20663503b64665ac0dbf3927ba72bb661f00c3b651cb645b94606bedeb1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e806aac-62fc-5c53-a15e-a2ce175dad25"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-48234"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-48372/ma-source-field/3ff34e1e0cc75c9b61493e82f0d09b5089e0827440681e783d8c3fbb165786f2/e1f821bff3e32bcda7ee7d2acc9e426a5a753b7b256a5501d0ce1d20a5bda7bf" :answer/id #uuid "7c5bdcf3-a541-5f6f-8726-df468ddf9f59" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.02\\text{ km}/\\text{month}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48372/ma-source-field/3ff34e1e0cc75c9b61493e82f0d09b5089e0827440681e783d8c3fbb165786f2/14bc67b7e38b2c052c4733b4bce02e6d254fda218336aa05c2e028b261f50d41" :answer/id #uuid "9c33f2db-ee69-5899-b254-c9920d0cc08c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.05\\text{ km}/\\text{month}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48372/ma-source-field/3ff34e1e0cc75c9b61493e82f0d09b5089e0827440681e783d8c3fbb165786f2/b93c3cf420a425b6411c3139ba44610b8e2992ebd3c4c7cc5029f7e8e17c86cb" :answer/id #uuid "df4e741b-904e-553c-b625-b7472668b725" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.1\\text{ km}/\\text{month}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48372/ma-source-field/3ff34e1e0cc75c9b61493e82f0d09b5089e0827440681e783d8c3fbb165786f2/874fd58cb2eca1d6694d5ba945287685db91fb08e85ff2bbb8feb4c314371bc7" :answer/id #uuid "0b0dea4f-92e3-5582-a8c3-1096bf841a98" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.25\\text{ km}/\\text{month}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48372/ma-source-field/3ff34e1e0cc75c9b61493e82f0d09b5089e0827440681e783d8c3fbb165786f2/7b01c9fabfde241ca062f40c2fbaf32a30d5a7ece0a3f326dce0777e7c35f2c7" :answer/id #uuid "07f94e7c-eaf0-50a5-82b2-936289c3244e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.5\\text{ km}/\\text{month}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48372/ma-source-field/3ff34e1e0cc75c9b61493e82f0d09b5089e0827440681e783d8c3fbb165786f2" :answer-field/id #uuid "aa8c5ef9-0c37-5adf-839c-99af73ffe32d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-48372/ma-source-field/3ff34e1e0cc75c9b61493e82f0d09b5089e0827440681e783d8c3fbb165786f2/e1f821bff3e32bcda7ee7d2acc9e426a5a753b7b256a5501d0ce1d20a5bda7bf" "q-48372/ma-source-field/3ff34e1e0cc75c9b61493e82f0d09b5089e0827440681e783d8c3fbb165786f2/14bc67b7e38b2c052c4733b4bce02e6d254fda218336aa05c2e028b261f50d41" "q-48372/ma-source-field/3ff34e1e0cc75c9b61493e82f0d09b5089e0827440681e783d8c3fbb165786f2/b93c3cf420a425b6411c3139ba44610b8e2992ebd3c4c7cc5029f7e8e17c86cb" "q-48372/ma-source-field/3ff34e1e0cc75c9b61493e82f0d09b5089e0827440681e783d8c3fbb165786f2/874fd58cb2eca1d6694d5ba945287685db91fb08e85ff2bbb8feb4c314371bc7" "q-48372/ma-source-field/3ff34e1e0cc75c9b61493e82f0d09b5089e0827440681e783d8c3fbb165786f2/7b01c9fabfde241ca062f40c2fbaf32a30d5a7ece0a3f326dce0777e7c35f2c7"] :answer-field/correct "q-48372/ma-source-field/3ff34e1e0cc75c9b61493e82f0d09b5089e0827440681e783d8c3fbb165786f2/e1f821bff3e32bcda7ee7d2acc9e426a5a753b7b256a5501d0ce1d20a5bda7bf" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-48372" :question/id #uuid "51c2bb08-d00d-5a09-b26c-2e5b5f8107fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-48372" :question/problem "![](images/4f/4f636c252da8e886c1f4bf9f0d87387339fce91e5a72f6275e6b24bba787d288.png)\n\nAnna is an environmentalist and is studying the impact of deforestation in a pine forest located in a square-shaped region. Anna discovers that the area of ​​the deforested region is decreasing at a rate of $0.4{\\text{ km}}^{2}/\\text{month}.$ At what rate are the region's diagonals decreasing if the diagonals are $20\\text{ km}$ long?" :question/worked-solution "Let $h$ and $A$ be the length of the diagonals and the area of the square, respectively. Using the Pythagorean theorem, it can be shown that the relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{h}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $A$ decreases at a rate of $0.4{\\text{ km}}^{2}/\\text{month},$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the diagonals when the diagonal has length $h=20.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{20}\\cdot (-0.4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{0.4}{20} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-0.02\\,\\text{km}\\text{/month}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the diagonals of the region are decreasing at a rate of  $0.02\\text{ km}/\\text{month}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-48372/ma-source-field/3ff34e1e0cc75c9b61493e82f0d09b5089e0827440681e783d8c3fbb165786f2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e2af960-841b-56a9-bc7c-099bf82e0fe8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-48372"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-49027/ma-source-field/88f76c4e58aa8d42b86cacff8ef50d40fd1a6ed5a0458954567198d10dac5420/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "5385b713-f0ec-57f9-9bc4-e473da877f68" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49027/ma-source-field/88f76c4e58aa8d42b86cacff8ef50d40fd1a6ed5a0458954567198d10dac5420/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "d5e7d0c1-250d-5300-a991-d81aee243a04" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49027/ma-source-field/88f76c4e58aa8d42b86cacff8ef50d40fd1a6ed5a0458954567198d10dac5420/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "2f831889-41ef-5e2e-b738-5ac690d2e875" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49027/ma-source-field/88f76c4e58aa8d42b86cacff8ef50d40fd1a6ed5a0458954567198d10dac5420/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "80391d2b-cd87-5377-b8af-b4ce9fbda203" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49027/ma-source-field/88f76c4e58aa8d42b86cacff8ef50d40fd1a6ed5a0458954567198d10dac5420/74053cd8f212994373f56db80fbf0eade2779b20bfbeb0bc87c25a146cc918d6" :answer/id #uuid "54bbe447-6b4b-5f58-83a2-faffcfe8e2db" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x)$ does not have any local maxima" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49027/ma-source-field/88f76c4e58aa8d42b86cacff8ef50d40fd1a6ed5a0458954567198d10dac5420" :answer-field/id #uuid "95508699-f6d9-5815-8c2e-e441b5ff17ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-49027/ma-source-field/88f76c4e58aa8d42b86cacff8ef50d40fd1a6ed5a0458954567198d10dac5420/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-49027/ma-source-field/88f76c4e58aa8d42b86cacff8ef50d40fd1a6ed5a0458954567198d10dac5420/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-49027/ma-source-field/88f76c4e58aa8d42b86cacff8ef50d40fd1a6ed5a0458954567198d10dac5420/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-49027/ma-source-field/88f76c4e58aa8d42b86cacff8ef50d40fd1a6ed5a0458954567198d10dac5420/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-49027/ma-source-field/88f76c4e58aa8d42b86cacff8ef50d40fd1a6ed5a0458954567198d10dac5420/74053cd8f212994373f56db80fbf0eade2779b20bfbeb0bc87c25a146cc918d6"] :answer-field/correct "q-49027/ma-source-field/88f76c4e58aa8d42b86cacff8ef50d40fd1a6ed5a0458954567198d10dac5420/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-49027" :question/id #uuid "d7fa8c48-a61b-5fbf-8ae6-af7bb5265a7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-49027" :question/problem "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{1+x}$ defined for $x\\in (0,\\infty )$ have a local maximum?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}})\\cdot (1+x)-\\sqrt{x}}{(1+x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1+x)-2x}{2\\sqrt{x}(1+x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-x}{2\\sqrt{x}(1+x)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ on $(0,\\infty )$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-x}{2\\sqrt{x}(1+x)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 1-x & =0 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=1$ is a stationary point. Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=\\frac{1}{2},2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\frac{(1-\\frac{1}{2})}{2\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}}{(1+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{9\\sqrt{2}}>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1-2}{2\\sqrt{2}(1+2)^{2}}=-\\frac{1}{18\\sqrt{2}}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\n- At $x=1,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=1$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-49027/ma-source-field/88f76c4e58aa8d42b86cacff8ef50d40fd1a6ed5a0458954567198d10dac5420"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dbad38a0-e6cb-563a-8e9c-1cee08397083"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-49027"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-49431/ma-source-field/47a046dd82f8bf343fcdb35445212357ef4b066169eeb04121d9ccfcc710a62e/59e257b97ed92e5e1f17b1f42e77c45c0f027805eadf620728a4d0b5f937db25" :answer/id #uuid "c966cc5b-2f5d-56fa-94a2-562519ed3dc2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{1-9z}+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49431/ma-source-field/47a046dd82f8bf343fcdb35445212357ef4b066169eeb04121d9ccfcc710a62e/36f43b5d1fdb762cc1e2a49ea0f72b0ea6b62bb4571f57f3d27879816e2df7cb" :answer/id #uuid "739b7d37-465f-53a0-92fd-a46f218e891c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{(1-9z)^{3}}+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49431/ma-source-field/47a046dd82f8bf343fcdb35445212357ef4b066169eeb04121d9ccfcc710a62e/7e4d24ea6896c16fb7fa14d971401602997a01d6b44e1f9f2d5882b84677eccf" :answer/id #uuid "570962e4-656c-562b-b969-b8505698bd15" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{1-9z}+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49431/ma-source-field/47a046dd82f8bf343fcdb35445212357ef4b066169eeb04121d9ccfcc710a62e/b92cddec5469d97b82ca517ff52bb4658d2d37c298c53b68ac55e5c190f8055c" :answer/id #uuid "c91eab72-8ce7-50be-b8c3-3258720d8056" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{(1-9z)^{3}}+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49431/ma-source-field/47a046dd82f8bf343fcdb35445212357ef4b066169eeb04121d9ccfcc710a62e/8833382ff5574c7ddf00b9d79f3706e221c5899f9d9e82d4cc0d51f475310541" :answer/id #uuid "bede226b-b6b5-5601-a51f-99f2392a59d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9\\sqrt{1-9z}+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49431/ma-source-field/47a046dd82f8bf343fcdb35445212357ef4b066169eeb04121d9ccfcc710a62e" :answer-field/id #uuid "f1f41607-89e1-5a62-888e-441db9b42ea9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-49431/ma-source-field/47a046dd82f8bf343fcdb35445212357ef4b066169eeb04121d9ccfcc710a62e/59e257b97ed92e5e1f17b1f42e77c45c0f027805eadf620728a4d0b5f937db25" "q-49431/ma-source-field/47a046dd82f8bf343fcdb35445212357ef4b066169eeb04121d9ccfcc710a62e/36f43b5d1fdb762cc1e2a49ea0f72b0ea6b62bb4571f57f3d27879816e2df7cb" "q-49431/ma-source-field/47a046dd82f8bf343fcdb35445212357ef4b066169eeb04121d9ccfcc710a62e/7e4d24ea6896c16fb7fa14d971401602997a01d6b44e1f9f2d5882b84677eccf" "q-49431/ma-source-field/47a046dd82f8bf343fcdb35445212357ef4b066169eeb04121d9ccfcc710a62e/b92cddec5469d97b82ca517ff52bb4658d2d37c298c53b68ac55e5c190f8055c" "q-49431/ma-source-field/47a046dd82f8bf343fcdb35445212357ef4b066169eeb04121d9ccfcc710a62e/8833382ff5574c7ddf00b9d79f3706e221c5899f9d9e82d4cc0d51f475310541"] :answer-field/correct "q-49431/ma-source-field/47a046dd82f8bf343fcdb35445212357ef4b066169eeb04121d9ccfcc710a62e/7e4d24ea6896c16fb7fa14d971401602997a01d6b44e1f9f2d5882b84677eccf" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-49431" :question/id #uuid "78d0396e-82eb-542b-a43f-b6df87f217ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-49431" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-9z}}\\,\\text{d}z.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int (1-9z)^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nNow, we let $u=1-9z.$ Then differentiating with respect to $z$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}=-9\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}z=-\\frac{1}{9}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $u$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (1-9z)^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int {u}^{-1/2}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{9}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}\\int {u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}\\frac{1}{(1/2)}{u}^{1/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}{u}^{1/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}(1-9z)^{1/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{1-9z}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}[-\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{1-9z}+C] & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}[\\sqrt{1-9z}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}[(1-9z)^{1/2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(1-9z)^{-1/2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}(1-9z)+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot (1-9z)^{-1/2}\\cdot (-9) \\\\  & =(1-9z)^{-1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-9z}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-49431/ma-source-field/47a046dd82f8bf343fcdb35445212357ef4b066169eeb04121d9ccfcc710a62e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ae5760b-9277-596c-8546-dca4fd985bd7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-49431"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-49439/ma-source-field/5c41871ef3df294e0835180881b2fbcb9ccaaa6ed37365d9cd8590292d661a82/c145fdb24ecb42784db713a129063e4c421b65db9b62ddf124f578b3913e3052" :answer/id #uuid "4061afb3-02dc-5f4e-ae1