6f99444a1d036e11e75aa0d6a340c5a566a93bd19f1/779b2c4342237363d51a202d156108c4eaa3f0f63a970b6cc8d7e3b84d1d028c" "q-76493/ma-source-field/0377ab3e5dd641539dc866f99444a1d036e11e75aa0d6a340c5a566a93bd19f1/0b9f20fc9d081bfd4f11325dcc088ae7fa3c02918af782dce61654380fb53a05" "q-76493/ma-source-field/0377ab3e5dd641539dc866f99444a1d036e11e75aa0d6a340c5a566a93bd19f1/593f36479e97b49088fe7879d160b4b7ec4317c69eae51df68dfc3167be40c2f"] :answer-field/correct "q-76493/ma-source-field/0377ab3e5dd641539dc866f99444a1d036e11e75aa0d6a340c5a566a93bd19f1/0b9f20fc9d081bfd4f11325dcc088ae7fa3c02918af782dce61654380fb53a05" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-76493" :question/id #uuid "21067b43-552c-57fe-bdc4-8f094139d29a" :question/math-academy-id "q-76493" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-\\frac{7}{2}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to the nearest integer?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{2}{7}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{2}{7})=106.601\\,549{…}^{∘}≈{106.602}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${106.602}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={106.602}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={73.398}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={73.398}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/e3/e378560486eca17f870b21743718c9f984f2bd3bf5609601f1cea674e15018e6.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{73.398}^{∘} \\\\  & ={253.398}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=106.602\\cdot 253.398≈27\\,013,\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-76493/ma-source-field/0377ab3e5dd641539dc866f99444a1d036e11e75aa0d6a340c5a566a93bd19f1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "387e57ad-6f80-5b90-8adf-7bf05618b532"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-76493"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-78500/ma-source-field/1e0e0f1f166578621589f9e2b75804631a62f96c25ce12ce05a9559bff17cdbf/eb3cd9e99842edb1208bec99aa7d747cb8011c78ace0517019b8154ca2a087fd" :answer/id #uuid "0ce29f2e-6690-5c30-addf-eb5b0269e1c4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78500/ma-source-field/1e0e0f1f166578621589f9e2b75804631a62f96c25ce12ce05a9559bff17cdbf/a3a5487afcc81a1c06c56ceaee82744a099a79debc8be0e8469f77e68bf12a04" :answer/id #uuid "40cf39a1-7eb8-57bc-a91b-26c7b8930628" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78500/ma-source-field/1e0e0f1f166578621589f9e2b75804631a62f96c25ce12ce05a9559bff17cdbf/f98c18a27dcb118caf5a5576c91aab2f7c6ef2dcdc9bdea8485ada011f865eac" :answer/id #uuid "600532cc-f1be-58a0-bec6-3ac60d7f098d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78500/ma-source-field/1e0e0f1f166578621589f9e2b75804631a62f96c25ce12ce05a9559bff17cdbf/82c79a11c5f316588a4977bf9de059017422d38604e630b6407357df3b622d75" :answer/id #uuid "36b35dce-f075-54ec-ba79-7120e2d404cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78500/ma-source-field/1e0e0f1f166578621589f9e2b75804631a62f96c25ce12ce05a9559bff17cdbf/d23151519d79f27e4cebc4ebad9047e36b8453244e6e3d202a56b90321183388" :answer/id #uuid "61db48ee-4b64-5a58-b44e-1d5c9b96cb13" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78500/ma-source-field/1e0e0f1f166578621589f9e2b75804631a62f96c25ce12ce05a9559bff17cdbf" :answer-field/id #uuid "4257f8e5-da5d-58bc-8b61-523a29fc6e71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-78500/ma-source-field/1e0e0f1f166578621589f9e2b75804631a62f96c25ce12ce05a9559bff17cdbf/eb3cd9e99842edb1208bec99aa7d747cb8011c78ace0517019b8154ca2a087fd" "q-78500/ma-source-field/1e0e0f1f166578621589f9e2b75804631a62f96c25ce12ce05a9559bff17cdbf/a3a5487afcc81a1c06c56ceaee82744a099a79debc8be0e8469f77e68bf12a04" "q-78500/ma-source-field/1e0e0f1f166578621589f9e2b75804631a62f96c25ce12ce05a9559bff17cdbf/f98c18a27dcb118caf5a5576c91aab2f7c6ef2dcdc9bdea8485ada011f865eac" "q-78500/ma-source-field/1e0e0f1f166578621589f9e2b75804631a62f96c25ce12ce05a9559bff17cdbf/82c79a11c5f316588a4977bf9de059017422d38604e630b6407357df3b622d75" "q-78500/ma-source-field/1e0e0f1f166578621589f9e2b75804631a62f96c25ce12ce05a9559bff17cdbf/d23151519d79f27e4cebc4ebad9047e36b8453244e6e3d202a56b90321183388"] :answer-field/correct "q-78500/ma-source-field/1e0e0f1f166578621589f9e2b75804631a62f96c25ce12ce05a9559bff17cdbf/a3a5487afcc81a1c06c56ceaee82744a099a79debc8be0e8469f77e68bf12a04" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-78500" :question/id #uuid "775b4967-50a1-57a8-aeb1-f88b0999b3dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-78500" :question/problem "Which of the following is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ for the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]?\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-I)^{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda ,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $k$ is a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)^{k}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-\\lambda I)^{k-1}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTo find a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to $\\lambda =1,$ we need to find a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A-I)^{2}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-I)v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each condition:\n\n- We start by solving $(A-I)^{2}v=0.$ We're given that\n\n$$\n(A-I)^{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,.\n$$\n\nThis means that *any* vector in ${R}^{3}$ is a solution to $(A-I)^{2}v=0.$ Therefore, the solution to $(A-I)^{2}v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={v}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $(A-I)v=0.$ We have\n\n$$\nA-I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,.\n$$\n\nThis matrix is already in reduced row echelon form. This means that the solution to $(A-I)v=0$ is ${v}_{3}=0,$ where ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are free variables. Therefore, the solution to $(A-I)v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={v}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the solutions, we see that the vector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ satisfies $(A-I)^{2}v=0$ but does *not* satisfy $(A-I)v=0.$ \n\nHence, $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ of $A.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-78500/ma-source-field/1e0e0f1f166578621589f9e2b75804631a62f96c25ce12ce05a9559bff17cdbf"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de97ba5b-7e05-5f3d-b11d-bf8e10c3bc54"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-78500"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186176576 :question/answer-fields 17592186175564] {:db/id "q-78501/ma-source-field/a977f33e9ed276295a791cb45465fda393ad652d8707c4c2196fe16e3c395815/a3a5487afcc81a1c06c56ceaee82744a099a79debc8be0e8469f77e68bf12a04" :answer/id #uuid "d4a0b590-4855-5213-be9f-29a1bf11e8f7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78501/ma-source-field/a977f33e9ed276295a791cb45465fda393ad652d8707c4c2196fe16e3c395815/f98c18a27dcb118caf5a5576c91aab2f7c6ef2dcdc9bdea8485ada011f865eac" :answer/id #uuid "21c2a1ab-5c06-5bdb-a757-d6d841d94d61" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78501/ma-source-field/a977f33e9ed276295a791cb45465fda393ad652d8707c4c2196fe16e3c395815/336c5b2664fa5df5a9af8176d64a36c85cb995c0b4fef5dcb4aff758c7b5b33d" :answer/id #uuid "4928ecd5-90a5-50cb-a6c0-17dc82d78bc8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78501/ma-source-field/a977f33e9ed276295a791cb45465fda393ad652d8707c4c2196fe16e3c395815/82c79a11c5f316588a4977bf9de059017422d38604e630b6407357df3b622d75" :answer/id #uuid "35168b7d-a9d3-5efe-bde4-cce3d83737df" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78501/ma-source-field/a977f33e9ed276295a791cb45465fda393ad652d8707c4c2196fe16e3c395815/7617091a43f8e032cf29d4f7b24700d7dfa70bc471479980d9abf828462de68e" :answer/id #uuid "60e8fd86-8b71-500b-ac59-c74ea61de3f0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78501/ma-source-field/a977f33e9ed276295a791cb45465fda393ad652d8707c4c2196fe16e3c395815" :answer-field/id #uuid "72f29203-29d0-5a2a-846c-4b037117267f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-78501/ma-source-field/a977f33e9ed276295a791cb45465fda393ad652d8707c4c2196fe16e3c395815/a3a5487afcc81a1c06c56ceaee82744a099a79debc8be0e8469f77e68bf12a04" "q-78501/ma-source-field/a977f33e9ed276295a791cb45465fda393ad652d8707c4c2196fe16e3c395815/f98c18a27dcb118caf5a5576c91aab2f7c6ef2dcdc9bdea8485ada011f865eac" "q-78501/ma-source-field/a977f33e9ed276295a791cb45465fda393ad652d8707c4c2196fe16e3c395815/336c5b2664fa5df5a9af8176d64a36c85cb995c0b4fef5dcb4aff758c7b5b33d" "q-78501/ma-source-field/a977f33e9ed276295a791cb45465fda393ad652d8707c4c2196fe16e3c395815/82c79a11c5f316588a4977bf9de059017422d38604e630b6407357df3b622d75" "q-78501/ma-source-field/a977f33e9ed276295a791cb45465fda393ad652d8707c4c2196fe16e3c395815/7617091a43f8e032cf29d4f7b24700d7dfa70bc471479980d9abf828462de68e"] :answer-field/correct "q-78501/ma-source-field/a977f33e9ed276295a791cb45465fda393ad652d8707c4c2196fe16e3c395815/f98c18a27dcb118caf5a5576c91aab2f7c6ef2dcdc9bdea8485ada011f865eac" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/id #uuid "22e82ef5-ade9-5251-8d75-4be1c8a1c5f9"] :question/problem "Which of the following is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ for the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]?\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-2I)^{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda ,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $k$ is a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)^{k}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-\\lambda I)^{k-1}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTo find a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to $\\lambda =2,$ we need to find a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A-2I)^{2}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-2I)v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each condition:\n\n- We start by solving $(A-2I)^{2}v=0.$ We're given that\n\n$$\n(A-2I)^{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,.\n$$\n\nThis means that *any* vector in ${R}^{3}$ is a solution to $(A-2I)^{2}v=0.$ Therefore, the solution to $(A-2I)^{2}v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={v}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $(A-2I)v=0.$ We have\n\n$$\nA-2I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,.\n$$\n\nThis matrix is already in reduced row echelon form. This means that the solution to $(A-2I)v=0$ is ${v}_{2}=0,$ where ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are free variables. Therefore, the solution to $(A-2I)v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={v}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the solutions, we see that the vector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ satisfies $(A-2I)^{2}v=0$ but does *not* satisfy $(A-2I)v=0.$ \n\nHence, $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ of $A.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-78501/ma-source-field/a977f33e9ed276295a791cb45465fda393ad652d8707c4c2196fe16e3c395815"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-78575/ma-sour