,\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}∗ & 1 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$   find the first column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$  \n\nSo, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=3.$ Computing $A-3I,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -8 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-80467/ma-source-field/3de1708500946b5b4069be057acc71002ee4ec56f4f8a266ceda0d99a56135d7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca18fa52-5494-53ad-bd02-208aba4ff9e1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-80467"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-80810/ma-source-field/75632f6a3a952c667732f1e5141a52b1ee09576ed