/answer-fields ["q-81402/ma-source-field/9916c635bf103df74e67fe846dc430ce738db7b9303ed935d687911f01fc3aee"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "93415651-fbcb-56d3-ad93-2b7f34e7bf4c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-81402"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-81772/ma-source-field/5298511b2dc127f087cdfc9a0eadaf180cd585cfe88562a98d03ad2bf5a9fdeb/dae510c648a25c4aa82ac48f3c72690ba39f8ef1db517987e9d562733826ca4a" :answer/id #uuid "7efe2cc1-7fd1-5257-b4ae-77bc8744aca4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1<t<3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81772/ma-source-field/5298511b2dc127f087cdfc9a0eadaf180cd585cfe88562a98d03ad2bf5a9fdeb/c6d3088f91d854a0efd39b315d86b5961c0e563abcef7292173dcecf14420feb" :answer/id #uuid "8f086f32-f9b0-5a9f-8ea4-43774d5f1175" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2<t<4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81772/ma-source-field/5298511b2dc127f087cdfc9a0eadaf180cd585cfe88562a98d03ad2bf5a9fdeb/1e8c68f257a459aba8351ca54d0d2c3a5b6f14510f14f6ba46dc13d38f4dff87" :answer/id #uuid "f6965c4f-a2b7-5188-a3a3-a57d5dc63484" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t>3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81772/ma-source-field/5298511b2dc127f087cdfc9a0eadaf180cd585cfe88562a98d03ad2bf5a9fdeb/f603345f1005561d843cb6a1fe8c9d2dc317d2c3e66627409de1ea5fd28461f9" :answer/id #uuid "1ab0b651-1995-5b9e-b502-fcc5bb486710" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$0<t<1$ and $t>3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81772/ma-source-field/5298511b2dc127f087cdfc9a0eadaf180cd585cfe88562a98d03ad2bf5a9fdeb/32a2a7ba31cfcac6170b525b8f97eb747e67862ae8f685f82d6de53089dfd24f" :answer/id #uuid "25e57da5-1b38-5f72-b567-d5c1afc8a6ec" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$0<t<2$ and $t>4$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81772/ma-source-field/5298511b2dc127f087cdfc9a0eadaf180cd585cfe88562a98d03ad2bf5a9fdeb" :answer-field/id #uuid "d3dd0609-1503-57fb-9ef8-371d237baa26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-81772/ma-source-field/5298511b2dc127f087cdfc9a0eadaf180cd585cfe88562a98d03ad2bf5a9fdeb/dae510c648a25c4aa82ac48f3c72690ba39f8ef1db517987e9d562733826ca4a" "q-81772/ma-source-field/5298511b2dc127f087cdfc9a0eadaf180cd585cfe88562a98d03ad2bf5a9fdeb/c6d3088f91d854a0efd39b315d86b5961c0e563abcef7292173dcecf14420feb" "q-81772/ma-source-field/5298511b2dc127f087cdfc9a0eadaf180cd585cfe88562a98d03ad2bf5a9fdeb/1e8c68f257a459aba8351ca54d0d2c3a5b6f14510f14f6ba46dc13d38f4dff87" "q-81772/ma-source-field/5298511b2dc127f087cdfc9a0eadaf180cd585cfe88562a98d03ad2bf5a9fdeb/f603345f1005561d843cb6a1fe8c9d2dc317d2c3e66627409de1ea5fd28461f9" "q-81772/ma-source-field/5298511b2dc127f087cdfc9a0eadaf180cd585cfe88562a98d03ad2bf5a9fdeb/32a2a7ba31cfcac6170b525b8f97eb747e67862ae8f685f82d6de53089dfd24f"] :answer-field/correct "q-81772/ma-source-field/5298511b2dc127f087cdfc9a0eadaf180cd585cfe88562a98d03ad2bf5a9fdeb/dae510c648a25c4aa82ac48f3c72690ba39f8ef1db517987e9d562733826ca4a" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-81772" :question/id #uuid "355a01e3-70fb-59a2-890d-575b2107fb75" :question/math-academy-id "q-81772" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=3{t}^{2}-8t+3,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  The positive direction is left to right. If the particle has velocity $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=1\\,\\text{s},$ determine the time intervals in which the particle moves to the left." :question/worked-solution "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3{t}^{2}-8t+3)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{3}-4{t}^{2}+3t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =(1)^{3}-4(1)^{2}+3(1)+C \\\\ 0 & =1-4+3+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{3}-4{t}^{2}+3t.$\n\nTo find the time interval in which the particle is moving to the *left*, we must solve the inequality $v<0.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{t}^{3}-4{t}^{2}+3t=t(t-3)(t-1)<0.\n$$\n\nPlotting the graph of $v(t)$, we get the following picture.\n\n![](images/9f/9f1dc2cc8c718f825c629bd92beb116326a36deb1dfbbe71e028cf1cf41445eb.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $v<0$ whenever $1<t<3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-81772/ma-source-field/5298511b2dc127f087cdfc9a0eadaf180cd585cfe88562a98d03ad2bf5a9fdeb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32c0fb55-c598-521f-aab9-72efc6b70789"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-81772"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-83144/ma-source-field/c26c6b658e9288298b556ac71ee1975824dfd3d9a6dd32a2e03f9bdf65d476d9/a6b3d9c589a1f61d3f71134b03d816f7b59de6e36e52207b18445684dadfc5d0" :answer/id #uuid "9c43e127-b476-5072-8782-64defe890eab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20\\,248" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83144/ma-source-field/c26c6b658e9288298b556ac71ee1975824dfd3d9a6dd32a2e03f9bdf65d476d9/4a5db5460effe4170eff073577d81804ebb0ed294d624f6eeda27590844181cb" :answer/id #uuid "34503797-dc86-5eff-9dba-4c2c0e4b5865" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "22\\,377" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83144/ma-source-field/c26c6b658e9288298b556ac71ee1975824dfd3d9a6dd32a2e03f9bdf65d476d9/1269238e825ab502eda5573fb9c9aac2dd2f753ad9896273ef9a196bb9c2c7f9" :answer/id #uuid "ad5e1f00-cc1f-5d4f-87c2-02b6b36f47ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "24\\,731" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83144/ma-source-field/c26c6b658e9288298b556ac71ee1975824dfd3d9a6dd32a2e03f9bdf65d476d9/465ef01d86d37637cc9db1d2b3a4299669a4b6350ae84494b27874168165e228" :answer/id #uuid "cec7db82-de58-50eb-a3aa-1156e21fbeca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "27\\,332" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83144/ma-source-field/c26c6b658e9288298b556ac71ee1975824dfd3d9a6dd32a2e03f9bdf65d476d9/d0d27503ee624c3e8f6074ad8aaad7e8850833c6ccd0b9a99e0571ed2bf96a5f" :answer/id #uuid "96af2a00-4233-59fc-a447-2f18d08406ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "31\\,755" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83144/ma-source-field/c26c6b658e9288298b556ac71ee1975824dfd3d9a6dd32a2e03f9bdf65d476d9" :answer-field/id #uuid "714a5ee8-c0ff-5aa3-a5d9-82c81856f170" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-83144/ma-source-field/c26c6b658e9288298b556ac71ee1975824dfd3d9a6dd32a2e03f9bdf65d476d9/a6b3d9c589a1f61d3f71134b03d816f7b59de6e36e52207b18445684dadfc5d0" "q-83144/ma-source-field/c26c6b658e9288298b556ac71ee1975824dfd3d9a6dd32a2e03f9bdf65d476d9/4a5db5460effe4170eff073577d81804ebb0ed294d624f6eeda27590844181cb" "q-83144/ma-source-field/c26c6b658e9288298b556ac71ee1975824dfd3d9a6dd32a2e03f9bdf65d476d9/1269238e825ab502eda5573fb9c9aac2dd2f753ad9896273ef9a196bb9c2c7f9" "q-83144/ma-source-field/c26c6b658e9288298b556ac71ee1975824dfd3d9a6dd32a2e03f9bdf65d476d9/465ef01d86d37637cc9db1d2b3a4299669a4b6350ae84494b27874168165e228" "q-83144/ma-source-field/c26c6b658e9288298b556ac71ee1975824dfd3d9a6dd32a2e03f9bdf65d476d9/d0d27503ee624c3e8f6074ad8aaad7e8850833c6ccd0b9a99e0571ed2bf96a5f"] :answer-field/correct "q-83144/ma-source-field/c26c6b658e9288298b556ac71ee1975824dfd3d9a6dd32a2e03f9bdf65d476d9/465ef01d86d37637cc9db1d2b3a4299669a4b6350ae84494b27874168165e228" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-83144" :question/id #uuid "c60fbb60-b78a-5c24-90ef-165c91d4dd65" :question/math-academy-id "q-83144" :question/problem "The number of subscribers $S(t)$ to a video channel after $t$ weeks can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=0.05S.\n$$\n\nIf the channel currently has $15\\,000$ subscribers, calculate the number of subscribers after $12$ weeks. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We model the number of subscribers using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=0.05S.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nS(t)={S}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.05t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${S}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that the channel currently has $15\\,000$ subscribers, so we have ${S}_{0}=15\\,000.$\n\nTherefore, the number of subscribers $S$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nS(t)=15\\,000\\,{e}^{0.05t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the number of subscribers when $t=12,$ we substitute $t=12$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(12) & =15\\,000\\,{e}^{0.05\\cdot 12} \\\\  & =15\\,000\\,{e}^{0.6} \\\\  & ≈27\\,331.78\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding our answer to the nearest integer, we conclude that the number of subscribers is $27\\,332$ after $12$ weeks." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-83144/ma-source-field/c26c6b658e9288298b556ac71ee1975824dfd3d9a6dd32a2e03f9bdf65d476d9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ba25c0c-9ba9-586a-b66f-7f07076d2827"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-83144"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-83477/ma-source-field/e15aa3980e973581b3107ffc08abdc430abb0b5426cd68af5d4ac003fc981600/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "db4a7149-d824-54c0-86ee-b21c01fb5cf4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83477/ma-source-field/e15aa3980e973581b3107ffc08abdc430abb0b5426cd68af5d4ac003fc981600/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "cd0d464b-7ff4-56b8-bcb7-57b51b72d32d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83477/ma-source-field/e15aa3980e973581b3107ffc08abdc430abb0b5426cd68af5d4ac003fc981600/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "f5969ae4-0089-56bd-bf20-0cf41f4333df" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83477/ma-source-field/e15aa3980e973581b3107ffc08abdc430abb0b5426cd68af5d4ac003fc981600/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "6e287a52-db21-559f-9d89-5103b7019f80" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83477/ma-source-field/e15aa3980e973581b3107ffc08abdc430abb0b5426cd68af5d4ac003fc981600/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "37d0fe1a-19ca-5f80-a487-821d0d2f05da" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83477/ma-source-field/e15aa3980e973581b3107ffc08abdc430abb0b5426cd68af5d4ac003fc981600" :answer-field/id #uuid "249bd719-2909-5d27-a78e-0ce0dd0aaab3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-83477/ma-source-field/e15aa3980e973581b3107ffc08abdc430abb0b5426cd68af5d4ac003fc981600/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-83477/ma-source-field/e15aa3980e973581b3107ffc08abdc430abb0b5426cd68af5d4ac003fc981600/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-83477/ma-source-field/e15aa3980e973581b3107ffc08abdc430abb0b5426cd68af5d4ac003fc981600/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-83477/ma-source-field/e15aa3980e973581b3107ffc08abdc430abb0b5426cd68af5d4ac003fc981600/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-83477/ma-source-field/e15aa3980e973581b3107ffc08abdc430abb0b5426cd68af5d4ac003fc981600/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :answer-field/correct "q-83477/ma-source-field/e15aa3980e973581b3107ffc08abdc430abb0b5426cd68af5d4ac003fc981600/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-83477" :question/id #uuid "97e3e680-a9e7-597f-a877-a0daf5245a87" :question/math-academy-id "q-83477" :question/problem "Find the global maximum of the function $f(x,y)=xy-x-y$ on the closed triangular region $D$ with vertices $(0,0),\\,(2,0),\\,(0,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}y-1,\\,x-1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}y-1=0 \\\\ x-1=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ y=1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(1,1).$ Since the point $(1,1)$ is not in the interior of $D$,  we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the line $y=2-x,$ in the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(0,0)$ and $(2,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,0⟩,\\,t\\in (0,2).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,0) \\\\  & =(t)(0)-(t)-(0) \\\\  & =-t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points on the open interval $t\\in (0,1).$\n\n- The line connecting $(0,0)$ and $(0,2)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨0,t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,2).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(0,t) \\\\  & =(0)(t)-(0)-(t) \\\\  & =-t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points on the open interval $t\\in (0,2).$\n\n- The line connecting $(2,0)$ and $(0,2)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨2,0⟩+t(⟨0,2⟩-⟨2,0⟩) \\\\  & =⟨2,0⟩+t⟨-2,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2-2t,2t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(2-2t,2t) \\\\  & =(2-2t)(2t)-(2-2t)-(2t) \\\\  & =-4{t}^{2}+4t-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,1),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (-4{t}^{2}+4t-2)^{′} & =0 \\\\ -8t+4 & =0 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation gives one solution. Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain the candidate point $(1,1).$\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, in total we obtain four candidates:\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(2,0),\\,(0,2),\\,(1,1)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(2,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(1,1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-1$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-2$ at the points $(2,0),\\,(0,2),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $0$ at the point $(0,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-83477/ma-source-field/e15aa3980e973581b3107ffc08abdc430abb0b5426cd68af5d4ac003fc981600"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82691436-7076-5c46-bb45-462ea9218db8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-83477"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-83480/ma-source-field/c777ba20cdd61eba9774ee49249bc7991b1d451a04711bb0e4daa06c34f2a789/074db705e16e1e57f8a2aff94b5fe57d6f39b7233e5e76f422f3f665da482360" :answer/id #uuid "88495c5e-727c-5bdc-8c66-f5f7a1da7068" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83480/ma-source-field/c777ba20cdd61eba9774ee49249bc7991b1d451a04711bb0e4daa06c34f2a789/171af9e1c9f732f0a40cb09e06b917bfb1d83ecb667e2e6fc9149e02322838e3" :answer/id #uuid "a9de477f-3e83-5cb8-ab8c-3547e66a97cc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83480/ma-source-field/c777ba20cdd61eba9774ee49249bc7991b1d451a04711bb0e4daa06c34f2a789/412b9ee8422a2c1cd2c9d97e8a0a65bdb10407afd20caaa31a1ca12e83a832db" :answer/id #uuid "89aba4dc-e9ba-5704-a200-86c4f6f35692" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83480/ma-source-field/c777ba20cdd61eba9774ee49249bc7991b1d451a04711bb0e4daa06c34f2a789/0e217de0f562a2169cf62f92e096d4956b843916c9efa189b0ce9e6dff99e8fa" :answer/id #uuid "973ba97a-cf4d-57f4-b6a7-6d7f845916bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83480/ma-source-field/c777ba20cdd61eba9774ee49249bc7991b1d451a04711bb0e4daa06c34f2a789/de0e2ca3bccfd81ed52dc353be6df152df6ee2e05a6b29884be5e2b0ea2076e8" :answer/id #uuid "1965bab2-64b2-5353-bc34-d0701180dfe3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83480/ma-source-field/c777ba20cdd61eba9774ee49249bc7991b1d451a04711bb0e4daa06c34f2a789" :answer-field/id #uuid "adc9838b-0769-5bf4-b3d7-c8c2300681dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-83480/ma-source-field/c777ba20cdd61eba9774ee49249bc7991b1d451a04711bb0e4daa06c34f2a789/074db705e16e1e57f8a2aff94b5fe57d6f39b7233e5e76f422f3f665da482360" "q-83480/ma-source-field/c777ba20cdd61eba9774ee49249bc7991b1d451a04711bb0e4daa06c34f2a789/171af9e1c9f732f0a40cb09e06b917bfb1d83ecb667e2e6fc9149e02322838e3" "q-83480/ma-source-field/c777ba20cdd61eba9774ee49249bc7991b1d451a04711bb0e4daa06c34f2a789/412b9ee8422a2c1cd2c9d97e8a0a65bdb10407afd20caaa31a1ca12e83a832db" "q-83480/ma-source-field/c777ba20cdd61eba9774ee49249bc7991b1d451a04711bb0e4daa06c34f2a789/0e217de0f562a2169cf62f92e096d4956b843916c9efa189b0ce9e6dff99e8fa" "q-83480/ma-source-field/c777ba20cdd61eba9774ee49249bc7991b1d451a04711bb0e4daa06c34f2a789/de0e2ca3bccfd81ed52dc353be6df152df6ee2e05a6b29884be5e2b0ea2076e8"] :answer-field/correct "q-83480/ma-source-field/c777ba20cdd61eba9774ee49249bc7991b1d451a04711bb0e4daa06c34f2a789/074db705e16e1e57f8a2aff94b5fe57d6f39b7233e5e76f422f3f665da482360" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-83480" :question/id #uuid "ae635709-cef0-5d90-bcf8-39aaac1bc474" :question/math-academy-id "q-83480" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -9 & 7 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -9 & 7 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -9 & 4 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-5{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -9{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{9}{5}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{5} & -\\frac{2}{5} & 0 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{5} & -\\frac{2}{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}.$ Substituting this in the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{5}(2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2{x}_{3}+3{x}_{3}) \\\\  & ={x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-83480/ma-source-field/c777ba20cdd61eba9774ee49249bc7991b1d451a04711bb0e4daa06c34f2a789"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4def2d61-0093-53cf-bcbb-1d2c3e6e1738"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-83480"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-83481/ma-source-field/58fc8e8418317c6b4de837fef913bbbae0890f2a6113b15a5437a706e046c37a/f9ba8df6b0717280f10c420a8809aaf270540eeb781f0c9993e96a763aaec3a3" :answer/id #uuid "5869b5d8-1b29-59f0-8e8e-a890cf28490c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83481/ma-source-field/58fc8e8418317c6b4de837fef913bbbae0890f2a6113b15a5437a706e046c37a/a51b5d98d135312d006e556f37cffdda39fee6cebfacdc46fdfba370ab35568c" :answer/id #uuid "946b0b50-f143-58dc-b90c-fba5b6dccff5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83481/ma-source-field/58fc8e8418317c6b4de837fef913bbbae0890f2a6113b15a5437a706e046c37a/2a2dfa122b83e1fbc6b70be6ef717447914d5f79f3dfea05d917ce624c516d65" :answer/id #uuid "1ff8c962-ac33-5ade-9011-9b96b78cc434" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83481/ma-source-field/58fc8e8418317c6b4de837fef913bbbae0890f2a6113b15a5437a706e046c37a/9d1e17d9784da5ae0067ade351923f4b0836785a26b2360fd2d3ae75fb99bcb4" :answer/id #uuid "3b5cfc50-7130-55a5-a100-906bbe37b827" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83481/ma-source-field/58fc8e8418317c6b4de837fef913bbbae0890f2a6113b15a5437a706e046c37a/097bf54e53fd44fd496a8806d7ef0ea4f567d8a5a994b298b0fe8b19f4b98622" :answer/id #uuid "5cf764b3-b5dc-5b5a-abc6-619ed240e8d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83481/ma-source-field/58fc8e8418317c6b4de837fef913bbbae0890f2a6113b15a5437a706e046c37a" :answer-field/id #uuid "f7423f8b-a007-5757-8b22-bf2bd8ad4565" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-83481/ma-source-field/58fc8e8418317c6b4de837fef913bbbae0890f2a6113b15a5437a706e046c37a/f9ba8df6b0717280f10c420a8809aaf270540eeb781f0c9993e96a763aaec3a3" "q-83481/ma-source-field/58fc8e8418317c6b4de837fef913bbbae0890f2a6113b15a5437a706e046c37a/a51b5d98d135312d006e556f37cffdda39fee6cebfacdc46fdfba370ab35568c" "q-83481/ma-source-field/58fc8e8418317c6b4de837fef913bbbae0890f2a6113b15a5437a706e046c37a/2a2dfa122b83e1fbc6b70be6ef717447914d5f79f3dfea05d917ce624c516d65" "q-83481/ma-source-field/58fc8e8418317c6b4de837fef913bbbae0890f2a6113b15a5437a706e046c37a/9d1e17d9784da5ae0067ade351923f4b0836785a26b2360fd2d3ae75fb99bcb4" "q-83481/ma-source-field/58fc8e8418317c6b4de837fef913bbbae0890f2a6113b15a5437a706e046c37a/097bf54e53fd44fd496a8806d7ef0ea4f567d8a5a994b298b0fe8b19f4b98622"] :answer-field/correct "q-83481/ma-source-field/58fc8e8418317c6b4de837fef913bbbae0890f2a6113b15a5437a706e046c37a/a51b5d98d135312d006e556f37cffdda39fee6cebfacdc46fdfba370ab35568c" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-83481" :question/id #uuid "a11ee4d1-3e88-5212-80d0-293758418f1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-83481" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 9 & -7 \\\\ -14 & 13 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =2.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 9 & -7 \\\\ -14 & 13 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 & 9 & -7 \\\\ -14 & 11 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-11{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}-7{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -14{x}_{1}+11{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 & 9 & -7 & 0 \\\\ -14 & 11 & -8 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -14 & 11 & -8 & 0 \\\\ -11 & 9 & -7 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-7){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{11}{2}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{7}{2} & -7 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{7}{8}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=2{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this in the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-83481/ma-source-field/58fc8e8418317c6b4de837fef913bbbae0890f2a6113b15a5437a706e046c37a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4def2d61-0093-53cf-bcbb-1d2c3e6e1738"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-83481"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-83505/ma-source-field/aba1cb1f646e118ff58858c858ffa16d79acd57a07ec92d823153b3dba60a434/2a7c1a99e9e23adaa901d3875aa3d18803784074d59c1f3334ed37864b04f786" :answer/id #uuid "6e7db313-b4d5-5153-bcfa-dde6e87c4ffa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83505/ma-source-field/aba1cb1f646e118ff58858c858ffa16d79acd57a07ec92d823153b3dba60a434/7161f3140020b0dd4c15ea58a14042b8788c2ceccaa922c051ca801a7112dea6" :answer/id #uuid "1cb2cae5-16fa-5704-83b1-6c31b690cd55" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83505/ma-source-field/aba1cb1f646e118ff58858c858ffa16d79acd57a07ec92d823153b3dba60a434/a65366fb2e333fe86ff259e927ae0a38521c97202f66a3c47e1eae1bc4f7deb1" :answer/id #uuid "e12f4381-2d6d-5cb7-86dc-4e456318069c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83505/ma-source-field/aba1cb1f646e118ff58858c858ffa16d79acd57a07ec92d823153b3dba60a434/411cb28cf567767eddcf31c7d43950f43e801a5fcf5b6ebc9eb75b97807e7f64" :answer/id #uuid "97309839-825e-57b1-95c6-7824e0a74346" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83505/ma-source-field/aba1cb1f646e118ff58858c858ffa16d79acd57a07ec92d823153b3dba60a434/ba556e7acb517f0757d7e8ff8c635d14d7a8f4df2c8d0cf8e776462b90909863" :answer/id #uuid "4958c111-ad18-51a1-bae1-88b3f960e07f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "No stationary points" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83505/ma-source-field/aba1cb1f646e118ff58858c858ffa16d79acd57a07ec92d823153b3dba60a434" :answer-field/id #uuid "7e571167-d51a-53d3-8b9f-8daca0904597" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-83505/ma-source-field/aba1cb1f646e118ff58858c858ffa16d79acd57a07ec92d823153b3dba60a434/2a7c1a99e9e23adaa901d3875aa3d18803784074d59c1f3334ed37864b04f786" "q-83505/ma-source-field/aba1cb1f646e118ff58858c858ffa16d79acd57a07ec92d823153b3dba60a434/7161f3140020b0dd4c15ea58a14042b8788c2ceccaa922c051ca801a7112dea6" "q-83505/ma-source-field/aba1cb1f646e118ff58858c858ffa16d79acd57a07ec92d823153b3dba60a434/a65366fb2e333fe86ff259e927ae0a38521c97202f66a3c47e1eae1bc4f7deb1" "q-83505/ma-source-field/aba1cb1f646e118ff58858c858ffa16d79acd57a07ec92d823153b3dba60a434/411cb28cf567767eddcf31c7d43950f43e801a5fcf5b6ebc9eb75b97807e7f64" "q-83505/ma-source-field/aba1cb1f646e118ff58858c858ffa16d79acd57a07ec92d823153b3dba60a434/ba556e7acb517f0757d7e8ff8c635d14d7a8f4df2c8d0cf8e776462b90909863"] :answer-field/correct "q-83505/ma-source-field/aba1cb1f646e118ff58858c858ffa16d79acd57a07ec92d823153b3dba60a434/a65366fb2e333fe86ff259e927ae0a38521c97202f66a3c47e1eae1bc4f7deb1" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-83505" :question/id #uuid "6c8cbd85-ace7-5114-80e4-3625b6d8dbe5" :question/math-academy-id "q-83505" :question/problem "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨8(1-2t)^{3},12(1+4t)^{2}⟩,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨-1,-26⟩$ when $t=0,$ calculate the times at which the particle is stationary." :question/worked-solution "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to compute the velocity of the particle.\n\nTo compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨8(1-2t)^{3},12(1+4t)^{2}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 8(1-2t)^{3}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 12(1+4t)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-(1-2t)^{4}+{C}_{1},\\,(1+4t)^{3}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(0)=⟨-1,-26⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =⟨-(1-2(0))^{4}+{C}_{1},\\,(1+4(0))^{3}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,-26⟩ & =⟨-1+{C}_{1},\\,1+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=-27.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨-(1-2t)^{4},\\,(1+4t)^{3}-27⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is stationary, we solve for the times at which the velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨-(1-2t)^{4},\\,(1+4t)^{3}-27⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(1-2t)^{4} & =0\\,⇒\\,t=\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ (1+4t)^{3}-27 & =0\\,⇒\\,t=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution that satisfies both equations is $t=\\frac{1}{2},$ so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-83505/ma-source-field/aba1cb1f646e118ff58858c858ffa16d79acd57a07ec92d823153b3dba60a434"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "02a9771f-af0a-5af7-94b7-9d6cb6f060d5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-83505"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-83826/ma-source-field/48feb339505fcbc26b2254e5b2458c8f5f03ff0c485d754698e603148c941d8e/f853eeffac47c628af4a2b7e7b0ecd024652f548b0b0007bc18dba21ad82ad7c" :answer/id #uuid "36a5002c-c0b9-58ab-acb7-d95d0cb5a0cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83826/ma-source-field/48feb339505fcbc26b2254e5b2458c8f5f03ff0c485d754698e603148c941d8e/25666b67a56594dd5245ec05e89d883db07d04362ab51dbc90c39ad20f7f9142" :answer/id #uuid "918b64ff-b732-50dc-9158-806369dd83c4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83826/ma-source-field/48feb339505fcbc26b2254e5b2458c8f5f03ff0c485d754698e603148c941d8e/3ecfc975b491d9d95179818d01cbe270cc6e504a74b30ee61d374b14e0de8442" :answer/id #uuid "ad6e5666-ea13-5af7-b833-a4815ec380df" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83826/ma-source-field/48feb339505fcbc26b2254e5b2458c8f5f03ff0c485d754698e603148c941d8e/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "3009444f-bfd8-532d-8128-d1d01ae05164" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83826/ma-source-field/48feb339505fcbc26b2254e5b2458c8f5f03ff0c485d754698e603148c941d8e/d3a7133ef1a1f6526aaec763d522f5ae075d8386a4606de90dcb93094f1d21d6" :answer/id #uuid "3576f6c2-83e5-556b-a02f-499e53dc7e8e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83826/ma-source-field/48feb339505fcbc26b2254e5b2458c8f5f03ff0c485d754698e603148c941d8e" :answer-field/id #uuid "c7146fa8-f4db-5ae1-a574-b50b6f3b88e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-83826/ma-source-field/48feb339505fcbc26b2254e5b2458c8f5f03ff0c485d754698e603148c941d8e/f853eeffac47c628af4a2b7e7b0ecd024652f548b0b0007bc18dba21ad82ad7c" "q-83826/ma-source-field/48feb339505fcbc26b2254e5b2458c8f5f03ff0c485d754698e603148c941d8e/25666b67a56594dd5245ec05e89d883db07d04362ab51dbc90c39ad20f7f9142" "q-83826/ma-source-field/48feb339505fcbc26b2254e5b2458c8f5f03ff0c485d754698e603148c941d8e/3ecfc975b491d9d95179818d01cbe270cc6e504a74b30ee61d374b14e0de8442" "q-83826/ma-source-field/48feb339505fcbc26b2254e5b2458c8f5f03ff0c485d754698e603148c941d8e/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-83826/ma-source-field/48feb339505fcbc26b2254e5b2458c8f5f03ff0c485d754698e603148c941d8e/d3a7133ef1a1f6526aaec763d522f5ae075d8386a4606de90dcb93094f1d21d6"] :answer-field/correct "q-83826/ma-source-field/48feb339505fcbc26b2254e5b2458c8f5f03ff0c485d754698e603148c941d8e/25666b67a56594dd5245ec05e89d883db07d04362ab51dbc90c39ad20f7f9142" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-83826" :question/id #uuid "6ee6c969-d319-57a0-b1a1-3435c9110a6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-83826" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=7{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-2x+6?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $6$ and the leading coefficient is $7.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }6}{\\text{factor of }7}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-7$ does not take the above form. The value $-7$ can be interpreted as a fraction $-\\frac{7}{1},$ and the numerator $(7)$ is not a factor of $6.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-83826/ma-source-field/48feb339505fcbc26b2254e5b2458c8f5f03ff0c485d754698e603148c941d8e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a75b47ec-8d5a-545e-b915-daca92320322"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-83826"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-84598/ma-source-field/00fc353648a53f69474e9590622ad472210e973b22be2430200704eb4718ce90/0d0e3c2720b7f2bee2f17ccfd4e5bf7fbaced5388e8f428c618eb75e5cdff84c" :answer/id #uuid "d0b3f1e3-8c79-5c21-af24-2422b0553cf6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84598/ma-source-field/00fc353648a53f69474e9590622ad472210e973b22be2430200704eb4718ce90/47b96760cfd6d2cbdf03244bd588d74dbf33ee1d071d44a0388bc9386a654f9c" :answer/id #uuid "597ee6d4-7ae6-5b66-8819-c98a309ce41b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84598/ma-source-field/00fc353648a53f69474e9590622ad472210e973b22be2430200704eb4718ce90/d41c569707208b4074f1b79adce4f658c6fc5d5535006b64e1fc034d4fb6c872" :answer/id #uuid "267b3288-c2ab-531e-a4ef-c630d0c9728b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84598/ma-source-field/00fc353648a53f69474e9590622ad472210e973b22be2430200704eb4718ce90/c2178d24b49491af60edf51be738e40548663117057101768b83d8d8712eccf8" :answer/id #uuid "e2f71d5e-7c62-59ce-b3eb-ece84bf182c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84598/ma-source-field/00fc353648a53f69474e9590622ad472210e973b22be2430200704eb4718ce90/dc35eaf03889b7cdf01af0446f93965eb83a685a5eb832bbe904f9541c4e5aaa" :answer/id #uuid "2edfc2e7-c27b-566a-a56c-76809ee37046" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84598/ma-source-field/00fc353648a53f69474e9590622ad472210e973b22be2430200704eb4718ce90" :answer-field/id #uuid "453d5361-60be-58a9-a449-bd8996b7f09f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-84598/ma-source-field/00fc353648a53f69474e9590622ad472210e973b22be2430200704eb4718ce90/0d0e3c2720b7f2bee2f17ccfd4e5bf7fbaced5388e8f428c618eb75e5cdff84c" "q-84598/ma-source-field/00fc353648a53f69474e9590622ad472210e973b22be2430200704eb4718ce90/47b96760cfd6d2cbdf03244bd588d74dbf33ee1d071d44a0388bc9386a654f9c" "q-84598/ma-source-field/00fc353648a53f69474e9590622ad472210e973b22be2430200704eb4718ce90/d41c569707208b4074f1b79adce4f658c6fc5d5535006b64e1fc034d4fb6c872" "q-84598/ma-source-field/00fc353648a53f69474e9590622ad472210e973b22be2430200704eb4718ce90/c2178d24b49491af60edf51be738e40548663117057101768b83d8d8712eccf8" "q-84598/ma-source-field/00fc353648a53f69474e9590622ad472210e973b22be2430200704eb4718ce90/dc35eaf03889b7cdf01af0446f93965eb83a685a5eb832bbe904f9541c4e5aaa"] :answer-field/correct "q-84598/ma-source-field/00fc353648a53f69474e9590622ad472210e973b22be2430200704eb4718ce90/47b96760cfd6d2cbdf03244bd588d74dbf33ee1d071d44a0388bc9386a654f9c" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-84598" :question/id #uuid "2e3e5aa9-3c15-5f62-9549-6ee08ad4a6f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-84598" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 6 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDetermine whether $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$ \n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -6 & 6 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-4-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ 0 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-4-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-2.$ Since all of the eigenvalues are simple roots of the characteristic polynomial, $A$ is diagonalizable. \n\nWe construct a diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal. Therefore, one possible matrix $D$ is\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNote that the diagonal matrix $D$ is unique up to the ordering of the eigenvalues. Therefore, another possible matrix diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-84598/ma-source-field/00fc353648a53f69474e9590622ad472210e973b22be2430200704eb4718ce90"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05cb6509-dc1c-53b7-a495-4cfec2abdfe0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-84598"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186097385 :question/answer-fields 17592186096204] {:db/id "q-84829/ma-source-field/ba8ac2e1498250f8444fc0328560f789a7a9930b27483d3a6ee5edb50276e745/228d7b42fcb34515143bb5c9b68cc81052255c360fff1053199a2147dbd7eb12" :answer/id #uuid "b3ebdac7-d16e-5300-a6fd-1a829dfad4ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84829/ma-source-field/ba8ac2e1498250f8444fc0328560f789a7a9930b27483d3a6ee5edb502