5db67f18ae8fc3dc" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-96752" :question/id #uuid "017b78ef-16f0-5056-9e83-f2f23609a4ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-96752" :question/problem "Which of the following is the correct symbolic notation for the sentence given below?\n\n*\"The function $f$ maps the set of real numbers to the set $Y.$\"*" :question/worked-solution "The sentence *\"The function $f$ maps the set of real numbers to the set $Y$\"* is equivalent to the following symbolic notation:\n\n$\\,$ $f:R\\to Y$\n\nHere, the domain of $f$ is $R,$ and the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-96752/ma-source-field/68d0461fbe20bf0fba36165a2af17287d11f87a1ce66cf21cfa1ca2adfdeb6ca"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fdf7e44-a092-5990-a10e-d48b175dcef0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-96752"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-97071/ma-source-field/8bb7a0d86d8cc50ff1a7d9a224e205bc19a21b6e7570b8bc6522aaedc774afdc/228d7b42fcb34515143bb5c9b68cc81052255c360fff1053199a2147dbd7eb12" :answer/id #uuid "0899c6c5-3e81-55cb-a780-9e9332201c4b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97071/ma-source-field/8bb7a0d86d8cc50ff1a7d9a224e205bc19a21b6e7570b8bc6522aaedc774afdc/c9ed31d2748699b4aed35187dd8d03b23917b58096f40ae61a4cf8e4895b9096" :answer/id #uuid "a6982ec5-f770-5406-8115-0f127674fbbb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-\\frac{2}{3}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97071/ma-source-field/8bb7a0d86d8cc50ff1a7d9a224e205bc19a21b6e7570b8bc6522aaedc774afdc/582cd71aeac5eafecb77c6f9f5c1bde2004ee7fc2a9336ab1f49a69e317721e8" :answer/id #uuid "10fc3ece-7797-5f53-8829-a11ecea3780a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=3x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97071/ma-source-field/8bb7a0d86d8cc50ff1a7d9a224e205bc19a21b6e7570b8bc6522aaedc774afdc/770e48394050f1cd126034e12257978ecdbe471bb38fa5fbc8c9558a9bfcb4f5" :answer/id #uuid "9292a981-b609-5970-917e-547c35bcf2a8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{3}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97071/ma-source-field/8bb7a0d86d8cc50ff1a7d9a224e205bc19a21b6e7570b8bc6522aaedc774afdc/07d4d169afe54b72e6508fedd544e5d357fac0ff0f623249ab3b7f264f513fcf" :answer/id #uuid "1dd28d78-6826-51c4-bb97-c5994d734c67" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{R}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97071/ma-source-field/8bb7a0d86d8cc50ff1a7d9a224e205bc19a21b6e7570b8bc6522aaedc774afdc" :answer-field/id #uuid "29f8391a-382e-52ac-9e3b-9db6b56f6633" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-97071/ma-source-field/8bb7a0d86d8cc50ff1a7d9a224e205bc19a21b6e7570b8bc6522aaedc774afdc/228d7b42fcb34515143bb5c9b68cc81052255c360fff1053199a2147dbd7eb12" "q-97071/ma-source-field/8bb7a0d86d8cc50ff1a7d9a224e205bc19a21b6e7570b8bc6522aaedc774afdc/c9ed31d2748699b4aed35187dd8d03b23917b58096f40ae61a4cf8e4895b9096" "q-97071/ma-source-field/8bb7a0d86d8cc50ff1a7d9a224e205bc19a21b6e7570b8bc6522aaedc774afdc/582cd71aeac5eafecb77c6f9f5c1bde2004ee7fc2a9336ab1f49a69e317721e8" "q-97071/ma-source-field/8bb7a0d86d8cc50ff1a7d9a224e205bc19a21b6e7570b8bc6522aaedc774afdc/770e48394050f1cd126034e12257978ecdbe471bb38fa5fbc8c9558a9bfcb4f5" "q-97071/ma-source-field/8bb7a0d86d8cc50ff1a7d9a224e205bc19a21b6e7570b8bc6522aaedc774afdc/07d4d169afe54b72e6508fedd544e5d357fac0ff0f623249ab3b7f264f513fcf"] :answer-field/correct "q-97071/ma-source-field/8bb7a0d86d8cc50ff1a7d9a224e205bc19a21b6e7570b8bc6522aaedc774afdc/770e48394050f1cd126034e12257978ecdbe471bb38fa5fbc8c9558a9bfcb4f5" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-97071" :question/id #uuid "fdcf8d93-f073-5ef6-b0fa-29dd34699bc5" :question/math-academy-id "q-97071" :question/problem "Let\n$T=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nbe the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Then $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $T$ has proportional rows, and yet $T$ is not the zero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=1$ and $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line that passes through the origin.\n\nMoreover, the line must pass through the origin $(0,0)$ and the points $(6,2)$ and $(9,3),$ which are defined by the columns of the matrix $T.$ \n\nComputing the slope $m$ of this line, we get\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{3-2}{9-6}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto the line $y=\\frac{1}{3}x.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-97071/ma-source-field/8bb7a0d86d8cc50ff1a7d9a224e205bc19a21b6e7570b8bc6522aaedc774afdc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "390ecc8f-2938-5466-b49b-964a67f79755"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-97071"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186125267 :question/answer-fields 17592186124172] {:db/id "q-98117/ma-source-field/7f21bd9f7f303846536645ba71b74e094dce33418a7f5b6dfb1d92bfa9e8bf2b/3bf4159c8badd80e39d28f29800f030cc54a2dc59c2ec680a2cdde0d1a55d892" :answer/id #uuid "53c09fde-7f5e-5f4d-945b-3d9da7879a03" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{f}_{x}(c)+2{f}_{y}(c)-{f}_{z}(c)=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98117/ma-source-field/7f21bd9f7f303846536645ba71b74e094dce33418a7f5b6dfb1d92bfa9e8bf2b/7450c545617c65d137894f8fb1b59a1cf0534f502127947cd2b8ddf63cc8ece9" :answer/id #uuid "7fc4ace7-5893-5557-936a-3e6b47c57856" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{f}_{x}(c)-2{f}_{y}(c)+4{f}_{z}(c)=-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98117/ma-source-field/7f21bd9f7f303846536645ba71b74e094dce33418a7f5b6dfb1d92bfa9e8bf2b/7f1b9474a91f507c0651573ea9258dd250e0162477427eeccd00edee488dbf78" :answer/id #uuid "fe7d2efb-7628-5876-815a-bcb3a7b6b5bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{f}_{x}(c)-2{f}_{y}(c)+2{f}_{z}(c)=3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98117/ma-source-field/7f21bd9f7f303846536645ba71b74e094dce33418a7f5b6dfb1d92bfa9e8bf2b/eccbc318a8d8e9cbe13f64fe4c58f162c876711e08abf316ebcfd9983a89dff5" :answer/id #uuid "6332e767-ea87-5d27-b265-0b9a23c53783" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{x}(c)+{f}_{y}(c)+{f}_{z}(c)=4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98117/ma-source-field/7f21bd9f7f303846536645ba71b74e094dce33418a7f5b6dfb1d92bfa9e8bf2b/26afa89e99f04757764d7fe2cff128b7b8b59b758af6ecd2331f72211c3b32b7" :answer/id #uuid "35cd8ad3-22e0-53ff-b0ef-fe55044a8fa3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{x}(c)-{f}_{y}(c)+2{f}_{z}(c)=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98117/ma-source-field/7f21bd9f7f303846536645ba71b74e094dce33418a7f5b6dfb1d92bfa9e8bf2b" :answer-field/id #uuid "bd3c8411-8b88-57aa-81a6-28759d5876e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-98117/ma-source-field/7f21bd9f7f303846536645ba71b74e094dce33418a7f5b6dfb1d92bfa9e8bf2b/3bf4159c8badd80e39d28f29800f030cc54a2dc59c2ec680a2cdde0d1a55d892" "q-98117/ma-source-field/7f21bd9f7f303846536645ba71b74e094dce33418a7f5b6dfb1d92bfa9e8bf2b/7450c545617c65d137894f8fb1b59a1cf0534f502127947cd2b8ddf63cc8ece9" "q-98117/ma-source-field/7f21bd9f7f303846536645ba71b74e094dce33418a7f5b6dfb1d92bfa9e8bf2b/7f1b9474a91f507c0651573ea9258dd250e0162477427eeccd00edee488dbf78" "q-98117/ma-source-field/7f21bd9f7f303846536645ba71b74e094dce33418a7f5b6dfb1d92bfa9e8bf2b/eccbc318a8d8e9cbe13f64fe4c58f162c876711e08abf316ebcfd9983a89dff5" "q-98117/ma-source-field/7f21bd9f7f303846536645ba71b74e094dce33418a7f5b6dfb1d92bfa9e8bf2b/26afa89e99f04757764d7fe2cff128b7b8b59b758af6ecd2331f72211c3b32b7"] :answer-field/correct "q-98117/ma-source-field/7f21bd9f7f303846536645ba71b74e094dce33418a7f5b6dfb1d92bfa9e8bf2b/7450c545617c65d137894f8fb1b59a1cf0534f502127947cd2b8ddf63cc8ece9" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/id #uuid "9a668edb-9a8d-5acd-b6b7-2627f5a72df0"] :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)$ be a function that is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where $a=⟨3,2,5⟩$ and $b=⟨1,4,1⟩$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively. If $f(a)=2$ and $f(b)=3,$ then what is missing from the statement below?\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that $\\text{_______________}\\,.$ *" :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(b)-f(a) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (b-a) \\\\ 3-2 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c),{f}_{z}(c)⟩\\cdot (⟨1,4,1⟩-⟨3,2,5⟩) \\\\ 1 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c),{f}_{z}(c)⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,2,-4⟩ \\\\ 1 & =-2{f}_{x}(c)+2{f}_{y}(c)-4{f}_{z}(c) \\\\ -1 & =2{f}_{x}(c)-2{f}_{y}(c)+4{f}_{z}(c).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that $2{f}_{x}(c)-2{f}_{y}(c)+4{f}_{z}(c)=-1.$ *" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-98117/ma-source-field/7f21bd9f7f303846536645ba71b74e094dce33418a7f5b6dfb1d92bfa9e8bf2b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-98765/ma-source-field/38f32412e8e6c23748c003e56538bfa8227b1489c3540d8dc0a228495db34990/0644d5aafcfc27fe2de79ef5283def17c4a0d1644cfb686a77a529abd80ae250" :answer/id #uuid "c77702da-2dc8-56b5-9db1-be0b111ea63a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98765/ma-source-field/38f32412e8e6c23748c003e56538bfa8227b1489c3540d8dc0a228495db34990/9a8b9f27c6464222a06118d2eba234224ddb1483ab88e190f300a70e7e3bbbaf" :answer/id #uuid "affdcd79-dc85-53c4-b26a-150695001bdb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98765/ma-source-field/38f32412e8e6c23748c003e56538bfa8227b1489c3540d8dc0a228495db34990/a3a5487afcc81a1c06c56ceaee82744a099a79debc8be0e8469f77e68bf12a04" :answer/id #uuid "675e4916-c043-5a86-94e0-b5ce7ed867f2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98765/ma-source-field/38f32412e8e6c23748c003e56538bfa8227b1489c3540d8dc0a228495db34990/4c22a7bc8e7836692695cb686ba405c69222960189c569b98a1356f01a46dcd1" :answer/id #uuid "cb23530e-d73c-5fed-bfb2-d794a091435b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98765/ma-source-field/38f32412e8e6c23748c003e56538bfa8227b1489c3540d8dc0a228495db34990/b2bf59e36269bd39fbd388f126de8bbc8e28e7459d74c720f82ef1601a11e20e" :answer/id #uuid "66a239a8-93d6-518d-96e1-a3a28f06e1cd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98765/ma-source-field/38f32412e8e6c23748c003e56538bfa8227b1489c3540d8dc0a228495db34990" :answer-field/id #uuid "5ca4d33a-4ff2-52b4-8aee-3fbddebc35d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-98765/ma-source-field/38f32412e8e6c23748c003e56538bfa8227b1489c3540d8dc0a228495db34990/0644d5aafcfc27fe2de79ef5283def17c4a0d1644cfb686a77a529abd80ae250" "q-98765/ma-source-field/38f32412e8e6c23748c003e56538bfa8227b1489c3540d8dc0a228495db34990/9a8b9f27c6464222a06118d2eba234224ddb1483ab88e190f300a70e7e3bbbaf" "q-98765/ma-source-field/38f32412e8e6c23748c003e56538bfa8227b1489c3540d8dc0a228495db34990/a3a5487afcc81a1c06c56ceaee82744a099a79debc8be0e8469f77e68bf12a04" "q-98765/ma-source-field/38f32412e8e6c23748c003e56538bfa8227b1489c3540d8dc0a228495db34990/4c22a7bc8e7836692695cb686ba405c69222960189c569b98a1356f01a46dcd1" "q-98765/ma-source-field/38f32412e8e6c23748c003e56538bfa8227b1489c3540d8dc0a228495db34990/b2bf59e36269bd39fbd388f126de8bbc8e28e7459d74c720f82ef1601a11e20e"] :answer-field/correct "q-98765/ma-source-field/38f32412e8e6c23748c003e56538bfa8227b1489c3540d8dc0a228495db34990/9a8b9f27c6464222a06118d2eba234224ddb1483ab88e190f300a70e7e3bbbaf" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-98765" :question/id #uuid "ea8f7f21-702e-5433-ab10-60c9a800aee5" :question/math-academy-id "q-98765" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the $3$rd column of the matrix $U?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeros outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=3$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=2.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the $1$st and $2$nd columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the third column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector\n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0,\\end{aligned}\\,‖{u}_{3}‖=1.\n$$\n\nSolving this system, which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{1}-\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{5}{3\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the nonzero normalized solution\n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrix $U:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{5}{3\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-98765/ma-source-field/38f32412e8e6c23748c003e56538bfa8227b1489c3540d8dc0a228495db34990"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b36c482-75a0-5413-8e3e-4a72e3ddb26a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-98765"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-98768/ma-source-field/998a658c355779a56830765a9eee383875c63190736028c2571784ef17d90d9c/003e03159af382809c52553d8c5103b1a8d7a1948ce3d500d5ff5b416e6167db" :answer/id #uuid "a220f89c-3d0a-5463-b736-64bfc352bad4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98768/ma-source-field/998a658c355779a56830765a9eee383875c63190736028c2571784ef17d90d9c/a3a5487afcc81a1c06c56ceaee82744a099a79debc8be0e8469f77e68bf12a04" :answer/id #uuid "e92e99cd-068f-5ddb-b8d3-1c72fa9118b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98768/ma-source-field/998a658c355779a56830765a9eee383875c63190736028c2571784ef17d90d9c/4c22a7bc8e7836692695cb686ba405c69222960189c569b98a1356f01a46dcd1" :answer/id #uuid "293c8357-8265-5b01-b470-7d517f25aa72" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98768/ma-source-field/998a658c355779a56830765a9eee383875c63190736028c2571784ef17d90d9c/63fc6e544aa866b1c2116b59f1739ece133a8fa2f9217599b08f20fc5533117e" :answer/id #uuid "35a514cb-7982-5a31-a007-45f908842ccc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98768/ma-source-field/998a658c355779a56830765a9eee383875c63190736028c2571784ef17d90d9c/b2bf59e36269bd39fbd388f126de8bbc8e28e7459d74c720f82ef1601a11e20e" :answer/id #uuid "63e3470d-66bf-5e47-8c08-2675fd0d4713" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98768/ma-source-field/998a658c355779a56830765a9eee383875c63190736028c2571784ef17d90d9c" :answer-field/id #uuid "b3279ea5-57df-56e5-a59e-a9baf5354ec3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-98768/ma-source-field/998a658c355779a56830765a9eee383875c63190736028c2571784ef17d90d9c/003e03159af382809c52553d8c5103b1a8d7a1948ce3d500d5ff5b416e6167db" "q-98768/ma-source-field/998a658c355779a56830765a9eee383875c63190736028c2571784ef17d90d9c/a3a5487afcc81a1c06c56ceaee82744a099a79debc8be0e8469f77e68bf12a04" "q-98768/ma-source-field/998a658c355779a56830765a9eee383875c63190736028c2571784ef17d90d9c/4c22a7bc8e7836692695cb686ba405c69222960189c569b98a1356f01a46dcd1" "q-98768/ma-source-field/998a658c355779a56830765a9eee383875c63190736028c2571784ef17d90d9c/63fc6e544aa866b1c2116b59f1739ece133a8fa2f9217599b08f20fc5533117e" "q-98768/ma-source-field/998a658c355779a56830765a9eee383875c63190736028c2571784ef17d90d9c/b2bf59e36269bd39fbd388f126de8bbc8e28e7459d74c720f82ef1601a11e20e"] :answer-field/correct "q-98768/ma-source-field/998a658c355779a56830765a9eee383875c63190736028c2571784ef17d90d9c/003e03159af382809c52553d8c5103b1a8d7a1948ce3d500d5ff5b416e6167db" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-98768" :question/id #uuid "4670b2c4-578c-543d-913f-4a2e02949175" :question/math-academy-id "q-98768" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the $3$rd column of the matrix $U?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=5$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the $1$st and $2$nd columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the third column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0,\\end{aligned}\\,‖{u}_{3}‖=1.\n$$\n\nSolving this system, which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{2}-\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the nonzero normalized solution \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrix $U:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5} & 0 & -\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & 0 & \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-98768/ma-source-field/998a658c355779a56830765a9eee383875c63190736028c2571784ef17d90d9c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b36c482-75a0-5413-8e3e-4a72e3ddb26a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-98768"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-99009/ma-source-field/fa58b6befc4c6088d0eaef4573ca4cbf0aa0aae95957368781391fd4921962f8/125323c1578fa12db3ef9f2f56c96b77b6f9c052aea0c24629400db3f5b1bf8e" :answer/id #uuid "badd1b57-14e7-5b56-b3e2-4f76bf483962" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+Cx+D" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99009/ma-source-field/fa58b6befc4c6088d0eaef4573ca4cbf0aa0aae95957368781391fd4921962f8/8703ffd5f81c67de754180beb16bc00f9a5b83cba1a8c98f77aa5e3839ac5bd6" :answer/id #uuid "a32a5598-4875-50b4-a207-219749ce447a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C+D" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99009/ma-source-field/fa58b6befc4c6088d0eaef4573ca4cbf0aa0aae95957368781391fd4921962f8/ab5c1ecd9ac0bb9558a714e926006ab82e40522cbc25ed0aae8752f1be1f4edb" :answer/id #uuid "39ea83b4-6e8e-5133-9645-9dd52053a38b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+Cx+D" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99009/ma-source-field/fa58b6befc4c6088d0eaef4573ca4cbf0aa0aae95957368781391fd4921962f8/2ca6dfeda5c06ea7ec223ee5cb0b022f1345410f74719679c91e546423ab2ebe" :answer/id #uuid "0cc1b791-33fe-5cbe-8db2-7cec317b3415" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y={x}^{3}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C+D" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99009/ma-source-field/fa58b6befc4c6088d0eaef4573ca4cbf0aa0aae95957368781391fd4921962f8/7d7311cccb2fd2ea842062fde00339b4d4f6d9c6561c0fe19cd3746cfe6a0367" :answer/id #uuid "9947a22d-6748-5635-9bff-c054c9d5412e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y={x}^{3}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+Cx+D" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99009/ma-source-field/fa58b6befc4c6088d0eaef4573ca4cbf0aa0aae95957368781391fd4921962f8" :answer-field/id #uuid "76d542a7-e1e6-5040-8811-6896030adc94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-99009/ma-source-field/fa58b6befc4c6088d0eaef4573ca4cbf0aa0aae95957368781391fd4921962f8/125323c1578fa12db3ef9f2f56c96b77b6f9c052aea0c24629400db3f5b1bf8e" "q-99009/ma-source-field/fa58b6befc4c6088d0eaef4573ca4cbf0aa0aae95957368781391fd4921962f8/8703ffd5f81c67de754180beb16bc00f9a5b83cba1a8c98f77aa5e3839ac5bd6" "q-99009/ma-source-field/fa58b6befc4c6088d0eaef4573ca4cbf0aa0aae95957368781391fd4921962f8/ab5c1ecd9ac0bb9558a714e926006ab82e40522cbc25ed0aae8752f1be1f4edb" "q-99009/ma-source-field/fa58b6befc4c6088d0eaef4573ca4cbf0aa0aae95957368781391fd4921962f8/2ca6dfeda5c06ea7ec223ee5cb0b022f1345410f74719679c91e546423ab2ebe" "q-99009/ma-source-field/fa58b6befc4c6088d0eaef4573ca4cbf0aa0aae95957368781391fd4921962f8/7d7311cccb2fd2ea842062fde00339b4d4f6d9c6561c0fe19cd3746cfe6a0367"] :answer-field/correct "q-99009/ma-source-field/fa58b6befc4c6088d0eaef4573ca4cbf0aa0aae95957368781391fd4921962f8/125323c1578fa12db3ef9f2f56c96b77b6f9c052aea0c24629400db3f5b1bf8e" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-99009" :question/id #uuid "99338d6e-762d-5e46-924e-c9b8ff055bcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-99009" :question/problem "Solve the differential equation $2\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=4x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2x+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow, integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2x+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int x\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})+\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int ({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+Cx+D \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+Cx+D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-99009/ma-source-field/fa58b6befc4c6088d0eaef4573ca4cbf0aa0aae95957368781391fd4921962f8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "192bb38b-b609-56ff-b560-becfc38ea070"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-99009"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-99204/ma-source-field/8ed647979d1e6c14bc72e17cb37d81fe9c2b89e716dae1d839d489a497bdc18e/a835e9a19410f9ea67ea9774df070473e15060f59f33a9cfad9eb5d178f9e638" :answer/id #uuid "08059ff9-d83f-540a-a482-2a8f806a18c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "105\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99204/ma-source-field/8ed647979d1e6c14bc72e17cb37d81fe9c2b89e716dae1d839d489a497bdc18e/ae88b0e4e39efaf2d30ebd55cc98cb3ed5733c3f224b932ff48a4224b22ca5a1" :answer/id #uuid "40d83586-f330-5ddd-a884-c230dd198024" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "125" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99204/ma-source-field/8ed647979d1e6c14bc72e17cb37d81fe9c2b89e716dae1d839d489a497bdc18e/bd6a8fadee21d3bae00ca3beb5bb88107d613102577da4647e80fb7b41fbe935" :answer/id #uuid "591f3fa0-6797-5340-8094-49d07c9c3d89" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "216" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99204/ma-source-field/8ed647979d1e6c14bc72e17cb37d81fe9c2b89e716dae1d839d489a497bdc18e/d144965a68a71154d2438737e51156d9918da199ac2f07b96e885a6c60965580" :answer/id #uuid "44d35100-e5d6-5e35-ac5b-f307d1753f6e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "256" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99204/ma-source-field/8ed647979d1e6c14bc72e17cb37d81fe9c2b89e716dae1d839d489a497bdc18e/f54ee287f31559da0166b1ce79ed1a7447405825f846c727aac2c733e9b87b3e" :answer/id #uuid "1396c469-16f9-566a-aa93-62900910c424" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "25\\sqrt{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99204/ma-source-field/8ed647979d1e6c14bc72e17cb37d81fe9c2b89e716dae1d839d489a497bdc18e" :answer-field/id #uuid "25347463-2d58-522d-8979-b8544d1335f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-99204/ma-source-field/8ed647979d1e6c14bc72e17cb37d81fe9c2b89e716dae1d839d489a497bdc18e/a835e9a19410f9ea67ea9774df070473e15060f59f33a9cfad9eb5d178f9e638" "q-99204/ma-source-field/8ed647979d1e6c14bc72e17cb37d81fe9c2b89e716dae1d839d489a497bdc18e/ae88b0e4e39efaf2d30ebd55cc98cb3ed5733c3f224b932ff48a4224b22ca5a1" "q-99204/ma-source-field/8ed647979d1e6c14bc72e17cb37d81fe9c2b89e716dae1d839d489a497bdc18e/bd6a8fadee21d3bae00ca3beb5bb88107d613102577da4647e80fb7b41fbe935" "q-99204/ma-source-field/8ed647979d1e6c14bc72e17cb37d81fe9c2b89e716dae1d839d489a497bdc18e/d144965a68a71154d2438737e51156d9918da199ac2f07b96e885a6c60965580" "q-99204/ma-source-field/8ed647979d1e6c14bc72e17cb37d81fe9c2b89e716dae1d839d489a497bdc18e/f54ee287f31559da0166b1ce79ed1a7447405825f846c727aac2c733e9b87b3e"] :answer-field/correct "q-99204/ma-source-field/8ed647979d1e6c14bc72e17cb37d81fe9c2b89e716dae1d839d489a497bdc18e/bd6a8fadee21d3bae00ca3beb5bb88107d613102577da4647e80fb7b41fbe935" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-99204" :question/id #uuid "8e5f2d5d-b1b9-503b-9dbd-36de49acff81" :question/math-academy-id "q-99204" :question/problem "Using the method of Lagrange multipliers, determine the maximum product of three positive numbers given that the sum of their squares is $108.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be three positive numbers. We wish to maximize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xyz,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=108.\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $x>0,y>0,z>0.$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=108\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨yz,xz,xy⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}yz=2\\lambda x \\\\ xz=2\\lambda y \\\\ xy=2\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first, second, and third equations by $x,y,$ and $z$ respectively, we get the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}xyz=2\\lambda {x}^{2} \\\\ xyz=2\\lambda {y}^{2} \\\\ xyz=2\\lambda {z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives $x=y=z.$\n\nNow, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =108 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{x}^{2}+{x}^{2} & =108 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & =108 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =36 \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(6,6,6),$ and the maximum value is \n\n$$\nf(6,6,6)=6\\cdot 6\\cdot 6=216.\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the maximum (and not the minimum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(10,2,2) & =10\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =40 \\\\  & <f(6,6,6).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-99204/ma-source-field/8ed647979d1e6c14bc72e17cb37d81fe9c2b89e716dae1d839d489a497bdc18e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "849449fb-bb08-54a3-b8ea-9f33ddd7a3e5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-99204"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
